const FIREBASE_CONFIG = {
  apiKey: "AIzaSyAg9OGSUbn7A04K5nRdStjzwGrO0nrvidk",
  authDomain: "ward-round-e4822.firebaseapp.com",
  projectId: "ward-round-e4822",
  storageBucket: "ward-round-e4822.firebasestorage.app",
  messagingSenderId: "353156608996",
  appId: "1:353156608996:web:1b8faf5f9fa01dafd48df1"
};


const THEORY = {

/* ================= PEDIATRIC EMERGENCIES ================= */
'emergencies': {
  intro: 'Examiners test this chapter as **algorithms and thresholds**, not prose. Almost every question is one of three shapes: *what is the next step*, *what number triggers action*, or *which sign tells you the child is compensating versus failing*. Learn the ladders and the numbers cold.',
  sections: [

  { id:'em-shock', h:'Compensated vs decompensated shock', w:'must',
    body:`This is the single most examined idea in the chapter, and it hinges on one counter-intuitive fact.

**Blood pressure is normal in a shocked child until very late.** A normal BP does not exclude shock. **Tachycardia is the earlier indicator; hypotension signifies that compensation is already failing.**

In **compensated** shock the body maintains BP by raising heart and respiratory rate, pulling blood from the venous reserve, and diverting flow away from skin and peripheries toward brain and heart — which is why the child is **cold, mottled and pale at the extremities while still normotensive**.

Why it matters: **compensated shock is reversible; decompensated shock may not be.**

**Capillary refill** is measured by pressing the sternum for 5 seconds — **prolonged if >2 seconds**. It is affected by a cold environment, so interpret in context.`,
    table:{ head:['Early — compensated','Late — decompensated'], rows:[
      ['Tachypnoea','Acidotic (Kussmaul) breathing'],
      ['Tachycardia','**Bradycardia**'],
      ['Decreased skin turgor','Confusion, depressed cerebral state'],
      ['Sunken eyes and fontanelle','Blue peripheries'],
      ['Capillary refill >2 s','Absent urine output'],
      ['Mottled, pale, cold skin','**Hypotension**'],
      ['Core–peripheral temperature gap >4°C',''],
      ['Decreased urine output','']]},
    qs:['p1-32'] },

  { id:'em-fluids', h:'Fluid resuscitation and the maintenance formula', w:'must',
    body:`Two separate calculations that examiners like to combine into one question.

**Resuscitation.** Boluses of **10 mL/kg** balanced isotonic crystalloid (Plasmalyte, Hartmann's) or 0.9% NaCl, **reassessing circulation after every bolus** to avoid overload. In **trauma**, replace with **blood and blood products at 5–10 mL/kg** instead.

**The 40 mL/kg rule.** A child needing **more than 40 mL/kg** of resuscitation fluid is likely to need **mechanical ventilation** — call someone with advanced airway skills early.

**A febrile child should not receive fluid boluses unless there are clinical signs of shock.**

**Maintenance — the 100/50/20 rule**, per 24 hours:
- **100 mL/kg** for the first 10 kg
- **50 mL/kg** for the second 10 kg
- **20 mL/kg** for every kg thereafter

Fluid of choice is **0.9% NaCl with 5% dextrose**, considering 20 mmol KCl per 500 mL bag.

**Total = deficit + maintenance − boluses already given.** Deficit is % dehydration × weight.

**Adjustments** — reduce to **two-thirds** where SIADH is a risk (e.g. pneumonia); increase for ongoing losses; reduce for oral intake; **extend replacement to 48 hours** in hypernatraemic dehydration or DKA to avoid rapid osmolar shifts.`,
    table:{ head:['Assessment','Body weight lost'], rows:[
      ['No clinically detectable dehydration','usually <5%'],
      ['Clinical dehydration','5–9%'],
      ['**Shock**','**>10%**']]},
    qs:['p1-24','p1-33','p1-23'] },

  { id:'em-bls', h:'Paediatric basic life support', w:'must',
    body:`The whole algorithm follows from one fact: **in adults arrest is usually cardiac (ischaemic heart disease); in children it is usually secondary to hypoxia** from respiratory or neurological failure or shock.

That is why children get **5 rescue breaths first**, and why the compression:ventilation ratio differs from adults.

**Do not shake infants**, or any child with suspected cervical spine injury.`,
    flow:{ title:'Paediatric BLS — trained rescuer, no equipment', steps:[
      {k:'step', t:'Approach with care', n:'Ensure the area is free from danger'},
      {k:'step', t:'Stimulate — "Are you all right?"', n:'Do NOT shake infants or suspected C-spine injury'},
      {k:'step', t:'Open the airway', n:'Head tilt + chin lift · jaw thrust if unsuccessful'},
      {k:'step', t:'Check breathing — max 10 seconds', n:'Look for chest movement · listen for breath sounds · feel for air'},
      {k:'alert', t:'Give 5 rescue breaths', n:'Remove any obvious obstruction first. This step is what differs from adult BLS.'},
      {k:'step', t:'Check for signs of life + pulse — max 10 seconds', n:'>1 year: carotid or femoral · <1 year: brachial or femoral'},
      {k:'alert', t:'Chest compressions 15 : 2', n:'Rate 100–120/min. Push hard and fast. Start unless there is a definite pulse >60/min.'},
      {k:'step', t:'If alone, give 1 minute of CPR before seeking help', n:'Use a mobile phone on speaker if possible'}]},
    qs:['p1-39'] },

  { id:'em-choking', h:'Choking', w:'high',
    body:`Branches first on **severity**, then on **conscious level**. The examinable detail is the age split.

**Abdominal thrusts are never used in infants** — the liver and spleen are relatively large and poorly protected by the rib cage.

**Blind finger sweep is not advised.** Remove foreign matter only under direct vision.

Food is the commonest cause of non-fatal choking, followed by toys.`,
    flow:{ title:'Choking algorithm', steps:[
      {k:'decision', t:'Is the cough effective?',
       yes:'MILD obstruction → encourage coughing, keep watching for deterioration',
       no:'SEVERE obstruction → go to next step'},
      {k:'decision', t:'Is the child conscious?',
       yes:'Conscious → age-specific thrusts below',
       no:'Unconscious → START CPR'},
      {k:'alert', t:'Infant under 1 year', n:'5 back blows, then 5 CHEST thrusts. Never abdominal thrusts — risk of liver and spleen injury.'},
      {k:'alert', t:'Child over 1 year', n:'5 abdominal thrusts (Heimlich) — fist in the upper abdomen, pull backwards and upwards.'}]},
    qs:['p1-38'] },

  { id:'em-status', h:'Status epilepticus', w:'must',
    body:`**Definition:** a continuous seizure lasting **more than 30 minutes**, or intermittent seizures over 30 minutes without full recovery of consciousness between them.

The aim of treatment is to stop a **"prolonged" seizure (≥5 minutes)** from becoming status. Longer seizures carry a **poorer outcome and become more resistant to treatment**.

**"Don't ever forget glucose."** Check it, and treat reversible causes — hypoglycaemia, electrolyte disturbance.

The single most examined branch is **step 1**: which benzodiazepine depends on whether you have access.`,
    flow:{ title:'Status epilepticus — escalation by the clock', steps:[
      {k:'step', t:'ABCDE · high-flow oxygen · check glucose', n:'Confirm the seizure is ongoing'},
      {k:'decision', t:'5 min — Step 1. Is IV/IO access available?',
       yes:'YES → Lorazepam IV/IO',
       no:'NO → Midazolam (buccal) OR Diazepam (rectal)'},
      {k:'step', t:'Step 2 — second dose of benzodiazepine', n:'Call for senior help · prepare phenytoin · reconfirm this is an epileptic seizure'},
      {k:'step', t:'Step 3 — Phenytoin IV/IO over 20 min', n:'If already on phenytoin, give phenobarbitone instead. Consider paraldehyde PR while preparing or infusing.'},
      {k:'alert', t:'45 min — Step 4. Rapid sequence induction with thiopental', n:'An anaesthetist MUST be present'}]},
    qs:['p1-34'] },

  { id:'em-anaph', h:'Anaphylaxis', w:'must',
    body:`**Nothing substitutes for adrenaline, and delay is what kills.**

Diagnosis needs **acute onset** plus a **life-threatening problem** in at least one system:
- **Airway** — swelling, hoarseness, stridor
- **Breathing** — tachypnoea, wheeze, fatigue, cyanosis, SpO₂ <92%
- **Circulation** — pale, cold extremities, weak pulses, low BP
- **Disability** — feeling faint, drowsy, coma

Skin changes (urticaria, swollen lips, tongue, uvula) are **usually but not always** present — their absence does not exclude it.

**Epidemiology worth memorising:** about **two-thirds of paediatric anaphylaxis is food-related**; most episodes occur **under 5 years**, but **most fatalities occur in adolescents**, classically with **milk**, then peanut and tree nuts. **Asthma is an additional risk factor.** No trigger found in ~20%. About **1 in 1000 episodes is fatal**.

**Mast cell tryptase** may support the diagnosis; **a negative test does not exclude it**.`,
    flow:{ title:'Acute anaphylaxis', steps:[
      {k:'alert', t:'Intramuscular adrenaline — immediately', n:'Repeat every 5 minutes until clinical improvement. This is the only thing that changes outcome.'},
      {k:'step', t:'Call for help · lie the patient flat · raise the legs'},
      {k:'step', t:'Once specialist equipment is available', n:'Establish airway · high-flow oxygen · IV fluid challenge · chlorphenamine · hydrocortisone'},
      {k:'step', t:'Monitor', n:'Pulse oximetry · ECG · blood pressure'},
      {k:'alert', t:'IV adrenaline infusion only if 3 cycles of IM adrenaline fail', n:'Under anaesthetic supervision'}]},
    qs:['p1-35'] },

  { id:'em-brue', h:'Brief resolved unexplained event (BRUE)', w:'high',
    body:`A sudden transient episode lasting **less than one minute** of one or more of: **cyanosis or pallor; absent, decreased or irregular breathing; change in tone; altered responsiveness**.

The terminology replaced **ALTE** deliberately — the label now rests on **the clinician's assessment** of the event, not the caregiver's perception that it was life-threatening.

**Low-risk BRUEs need only observation and monitoring of vital signs**, a detailed explanation to caregivers, and an offer of basic life support training. An ECG, pernasal swab for pertussis and continuous pulse oximetry *may* be considered — **other laboratory and imaging studies are unlikely to help and should not be performed.**

**Failing any one criterion** means the infant needs fuller observation and investigation.`,
    table:{ head:['Criteria for a LOW-RISK BRUE — all six must be met'], rows:[
      ['Age **>60 days**'],
      ['Born at **≥32 weeks** gestation'],
      ['**First** BRUE'],
      ['Event lasted **<1 minute**'],
      ['**No CPR** given by a trained medical provider'],
      ['**No concerning features** in history or examination']]},
    qs:['p1-36'] },

  { id:'em-sids', h:'Sudden unexpected death in infancy', w:'high',
    body:`**SIDS is the most common cause of death between 1 month and 1 year.**

**Peak age 2–4 months**, occurring throughout the first year; **55% are boys**. Risk is increased **fivefold** by **low birthweight** and **fivefold** by **maternal age under 20**.

In most cases no cause is found even after detailed autopsy. Occasionally an undiagnosed congenital abnormality or inborn error of metabolism is identified.

**The "Back to Sleep" campaign is the single most effective intervention** — UK incidence fell from **1.9 per 1000 live births in 1989 to 0.3 per 1000 in 2018**.

**Safe sleep advice:** back to sleep, not front or side · feet at the foot of the cot, head uncovered, blanket tucked under the arms or a baby sleeping bag · **firm flat mattress, clear sleep space — no pillows or duvets** · separate cot in the parents' room for the first 6 months · avoid overheating, room at **18°C** · no smoking in pregnancy or after · **avoid co-sleeping**, especially after alcohol or sedatives or if a smoker · breastfeed · seek advice promptly if unwell.`,
    qs:['p1-37'] },

  { id:'em-sepsis', h:'Sepsis', w:'high',
    body:`Bacteria proliferate in the bloodstream; the host response releases inflammatory cytokines and activates endothelium. **Dysregulation of that host response is what produces the clinical syndrome.**

**Severe hypovolaemia in sepsis comes from "capillary leak"** — maldistribution of intravascular protein and fluid caused by endothelial dysfunction. Two consequences follow directly:
- **Capillary leak into the lungs → pulmonary oedema → respiratory failure**
- **Cytokines and toxins depress myocardial contractility → inotropes often required**

**Antibiotics must be started without delay** — beyond the neonatal period usually broad-spectrum, e.g. **ceftriaxone**. Neutropenia or cystic fibrosis need extended-spectrum cover.

**Neonatal sepsis** splits by timing: **early-onset — group B streptococcus and E. coli**; **late-onset — coagulase-negative staphylococcus**. Note that **CoNS on a blood culture is usually a skin contaminant** rather than true infection.

**DIC** — widespread inflammation causes microvascular thrombosis, consuming platelets and clotting factors, producing subcutaneous bleeding and a **non-blanching rash**. Correct with fresh frozen plasma, cryoprecipitate and platelets.

Immunisation has reduced sepsis from **Hib, meningococcus and pneumococcus**.`,
    qs:[] },

  { id:'em-abcde', h:'The ABCDE primary assessment and AVPU', w:'know',
    body:`**Airway · Breathing · Circulation · Disability · Exposure.** The primary survey exists to identify respiratory, circulatory or neurological failure. Resuscitate as you go, then perform the secondary assessment.

**Do not omit neurological status** — reduced consciousness itself threatens the airway, and some causes of coma are reversible (hypoglycaemia, opiate toxicity).

**AVPU:** **A**lert · responds to **V**oice · responds to **P**ain · **U**nresponsive. **A score of P means the airway is at risk** and needs a manoeuvre or adjunct. Equivalently, **GCS <8 means the airway is at risk.**

**Pupils in coma:** pinpoint and fixed → opiates/barbiturates, pontine lesion · fixed and dilated → severe hypoxia, during/post seizure, anticholinergics, hypothermia · **unilateral dilated pupil → expanding ipsilateral lesion, tentorial herniation, third nerve lesion**.

**Respiratory distress in infants** — moderate: tachycardia, RR >50, nasal flaring, grunting, accessory muscles, recession, head retraction, unable to feed. **Severe: cyanosis, exhaustion, reduced conscious level, saturation <92% despite oxygen, rising pCO₂.** Give supplementary oxygen when **SpO₂ <92%**.`,
    table:{ head:['Age','Heart rate','Respiratory rate','Systolic BP'], rows:[
      ['Newborn (0–1 mo)','100–160','35–60','50–70'],
      ['Infant (1–12 mo)','80–140','30–40','70–100'],
      ['Toddler (1–3 y)','80–130','20–30','70–110'],
      ['Preschool (3–6 y)','80–110','20–30','80–110'],
      ['School age (6–12 y)','70–100','20–25','80–120'],
      ['Adolescent (12+ y)','60–90','15–20','100–120']]},
    qs:[] }
  ]
},

/* ================= GROWTH AND PUBERTY ================= */
'growth-puberty': {
  intro: 'Questions here are almost always **pattern recognition between look-alike causes**, or **a calculation**. The examiner gives you a bone age, a parental history and a weight-versus-height relationship, and expects you to name the diagnosis. Learn the discriminating features in the table below and the formulas exactly.',
  sections: [

  { id:'gp-short', h:'Short stature — telling the causes apart', w:'must',
    body:`Every short-stature question is answered by the same three discriminators: **bone age**, **the weight-to-height relationship**, and **the family history**.

Two normal variants account for most cases:

**Constitutional delay of growth and puberty** — a normal variant, far commoner in boys. Slow *tempo* of maturation: delayed bone age, delayed puberty, **a family history of late maturation**, and — crucially — **a normal final adult height**, because the epiphyses stay open longer. Where self-esteem is significantly affected, a short course of low-dose testosterone accelerates growth and puberty.

**Familial short stature** — the child is short because the parents are short. **Bone age equals chronological age**, growth velocity is normal, puberty is normally timed, and **final height is short** but appropriate to the mid-parental centile.

Then the pathological causes, each with a give-away:
- **Hypothyroidism** — the **commonest endocrine cause**. Short *and relatively overweight*, markedly delayed bone age.
- **GH deficiency** — also short and overweight-for-height, **falling across centiles**. Screen with **IGF-1**; provocation testing only if growth is slow *and* IGF-1 is low.
- **Coeliac disease** — may present with slow growth alone; **weight centile falls below the height centile**.
- **Emotional deprivation** — short *and underweight* with delayed puberty; improves in a nurturing environment.
- **Cushing / steroid excess** — short with relative overweight; uncommon.
- **Skeletal dysplasia or storage disorder** — the only group with **disproportion**.

**Check a karyotype in every girl with significant short stature** to exclude **Turner syndrome (45,XO)** — dysmorphic features are often subtle, and **recurrent otitis media** is a supportive clue. Short stature in Turner is attributed to loss of one **SHOX** copy.

**MRI brain** only if there is headache or neurological signs (craniopharyngioma).`,
    table:{ head:['Cause','Bone age','Weight vs height','Final height'], rows:[
      ['**Constitutional delay**','**Delayed**','Proportionate','**Normal**'],
      ['**Familial short stature**','**Normal**','Proportionate','Short'],
      ['Hypothyroidism','Markedly delayed','**Overweight** for height','Reduced if untreated'],
      ['GH deficiency','Markedly delayed','**Overweight** for height','Reduced if untreated'],
      ['Coeliac disease','Delayed','**Underweight** for height','Normal if treated'],
      ['Emotional deprivation','Delayed','Underweight','Improves in better environment'],
      ['Skeletal dysplasia','Variable','**Disproportionate**','Short']]},
    qs:['p1-1','p1-6','p1-9'] },

  { id:'gp-measure', h:'Measuring, plotting and the mid-parental height', w:'must',
    body:`**Mid-parental height** — the calculation examiners set most often:

> Mean of the two parents' heights, **+7 cm for a boy**, **−7 cm for a girl**.

The commonest error is omitting the ±7. Most children reach a height within **two centile spaces** of the resulting mid-parental centile; the further a child sits from it, the more likely a pathological cause.

**Correct preterm infants for gestational age before interpreting anything.** Plot at actual age, then shift **back by the number of weeks born early**. Continue for the **first year**, or to **2 years** if extremely preterm. An infant born at 30 weeks plots 10 weeks to the left — a plot that looks like faltering growth often becomes entirely normal after correction.

**Centiles.** Height is normally distributed. Below the mean: **25th, 9th, 2nd, 0.4th**. Above: **75th, 91st, 98th, 99.6th**. Values **below the 0.4th or above the 99.6th** occur by chance in only **4 per 1000 children**.

**Disproportion** is assessed by measuring **sitting height** and subtracting it from total height to get leg length:
- **Skeletal dysplasia → legs short relative to the back**
- **Storage disorder → back short relative to the legs**`,
    qs:['p1-2','p1-3'] },

  { id:'gp-rules', h:'Growth rules of thumb', w:'high',
    body:`Pure recall, and easy marks if you have memorised them.

**Weight.** Gain decelerates through infancy in three 4-month blocks: **¾ kg/month**, then **½ kg/month**, then **¼ kg/month** — about **6 kg over the first year**.
- **Birth weight doubles by 4–5 months**
- **Birth weight triples by 12 months**
- An initial loss of **5–10% of birthweight** in the first days is physiological, **regained by 2 weeks**. Loss **>10%**, or failure to regain by **14 days**, needs assessment.
- Rough estimate: **weight (kg) = (age in years × 2) + 8**

**Height.**
- **Doubles at 4 years**, **triples at 12 years**
- Rough estimate: **height (cm) = (age in years × 5) + 80**

**The four phases of growth** and what drives each — this explains *why* a given deficiency produces a given pattern.`,
    table:{ head:['Phase','Share of adult height','Main determinant'], rows:[
      ['**Fetal** (8–40 weeks)','**30%**','Uterine size, placental nutrition, maternal factors'],
      ['**Infantile**','**15%**','**Nutrition**, good health, thyroid hormone'],
      ['**Childhood**','**40%**','**Growth hormone** and thyroid'],
      ['**Pubertal**','**15%**','**Sex steroids** plus GH']]},
    qs:['p1-4'] },

  { id:'gp-puberty', h:'Puberty — sequence, timing and the growth spurt', w:'must',
    body:`Know the **first sign** in each sex and the **position of menarche** in the sequence — those two facts answer most puberty questions.

**Girls.** First sign is the **breast bud (thelarche)**. Pubic hair (adrenarche) and the height spurt follow. **Menarche comes late — about 2.5 years after pubertal onset — and only about 5 cm of growth remains after it.** That figure is the one examiners want when counselling about final height.

**Boys.** First sign is **testicular enlargement above 4 mL**. The height spurt begins much later, at a testicular volume of **12–15 mL**, roughly **18 months after** pubertal onset.

**Why men end up taller:** boys have a **longer pre-pubertal childhood growth phase** and a **higher peak pubertal height velocity**. This is also the reasoning behind the ±7 cm in the mid-parental calculation.`,
    qs:['p1-7'] },

  { id:'gp-precocious', h:'Precocious puberty and the benign normal variants', w:'high',
    body:`**Precocious puberty** is pubertal development before **8 years in girls** or **9 years in boys**. The discriminator from the benign variants is simple: **is there growth acceleration, and is more than one pubertal feature present?**

**Premature thelarche** — isolated breast development, typically **6 months to 2 years**, from transient activation of the hypothalamic–pituitary axis. It is **often asymmetrical and fluctuates in size**, rarely progresses beyond stage 3, has **no other pubertal features and no growth acceleration**, and is **self-limiting**. **Investigations are not usually required** — reassurance and observation only.

**Premature adrenarche** — isolated pubic hair with no other features. Measure **DHEA and 17-hydroxyprogesterone** only if there is **rapid growth or significant virilisation**, to exclude excess adrenal androgen. Associated with later **PCOS** risk.

**Central (gonadotrophin-dependent) precocious puberty** activates the full sequence with growth acceleration; treated with **GnRH analogues**. **Cranial imaging is more important in boys**, where an intracranial lesion is more likely to be responsible.`,
    qs:['p1-8'] },

  { id:'gp-head', h:'Head circumference and skull shape', w:'high',
    body:`**Microcephaly** is below the 2nd centile; **macrocephaly** above the 98th. The **posterior fontanelle** closes by about **8 weeks**, the **anterior fontanelle** by **12–18 months**. A **tense fontanelle when the infant is not crying** suggests raised intracranial pressure — arrange cranial ultrasound.

The examinable discrimination is **positional plagiocephaly versus lambdoid craniosynostosis**, and it turns on **which way the ear is displaced**.

**Occipital plagiocephaly** — a **parallelogram-shaped head** with occipital flattening and the **ipsilateral ear displaced ANTERIORLY**. All sutures open, head circumference normal, resolves spontaneously with mobility. Became commoner after supine sleeping advice, and is also seen in **hypotonic infants** who move their heads less. Positional management and reassurance.

**Lambdoid craniosynostosis** — the mimic. The ear on the affected side is displaced **POSTERIORLY**, and a **fused, palpable ridge** is present over the suture.

**Sagittal synostosis** is the commonest localised craniosynostosis — a **long narrow skull (scaphocephaly)**. **Crouzon syndrome** is syndromic, with shallow orbits and **exophthalmos** threatening vision.`,
    qs:['p1-5'] }
  ]
}

};

/* ============ DATA ============ */
const QUESTIONS = [{"id": "p1-1", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "growth-puberty", "stem": "A 14-year-old boy is brought to clinic because he is the shortest in his class and is being teased. He is otherwise well, with no gastrointestinal or respiratory symptoms. His height is on the 2nd centile; his weight is on the 9th centile. His father reports he was still growing when he left school at 16, and his mother's menarche was at 13 years. Examination shows stage 1 pubic hair and testicular volumes of 4 mL bilaterally. Bone age is delayed by 20 months.\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely explanation for this patient's short stature?", "options": ["Constitutional delay of growth and puberty", "Familial short stature", "Growth hormone deficiency", "Hypothyroidism", "Coeliac disease"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Constitutional delay of growth and puberty is a normal variant, far more common in boys, in which the tempo of maturation is slow. The diagnosis rests on a cluster of findings, all present here: short stature emerging in the teenage years, delayed onset of puberty (testicular volume 4 mL and PH1 at 14 years is at the very lower limit), a **delayed bone age**, and a **family history of delayed maturation** in either parent — the father still growing at 16 and relatively late maternal menarche. Because the epiphyses remain open longer, growth continues after peers have stopped and **final adult height is normal for the family**. Where mood or self-esteem is significantly affected, a short course of low-dose testosterone accelerates growth and pubertal progression, after which puberty continues spontaneously.\n\n*(B)* In familial short stature the child is short because the parents are short. The distinguishing features are the opposite of this case: **bone age equals chronological age**, growth velocity is normal, puberty occurs at a normal time, and final height is short (appropriate to the mid-parental centile). This boy's parents are not described as short, and his bone age is delayed.\n\n*(C)* GH deficiency produces a slow growth velocity with **falling across height centiles** and children who are characteristically **overweight relative to their height**. Bone age is markedly (not mildly) delayed. This boy's weight centile is above his height centile only marginally and he is well.\n\n*(D)* Acquired hypothyroidism is the most common endocrine cause of short stature and, like GH deficiency, causes a child to be short *and relatively overweight* with a markedly delayed bone age. There are no other hypothyroid features here, and the family history points elsewhere.\n\n*(E)* Coeliac disease may present with slow growth as the only feature, but the weight centile characteristically falls **below** the height centile (underweight for height), and bone age delay would be accompanied by nutritional signs. His weight centile is above his height centile.", "objective": "Constitutional delay of growth and puberty is characterised by delayed bone age, delayed puberty, a positive family history of late maturation, and a **normal final adult height**. Familial short stature has a **normal bone age**, normal pubertal timing, and a short final height.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-2", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "growth-puberty", "stem": "A 6-year-old boy is seen for a routine review. His father's height is 182 cm and his mother's is 162 cm. The clinician wishes to determine whether the boy's current height centile is appropriate for his genetic potential.\n\nWhich of the following best represents his mid-parental target height?", "options": ["165 cm", "168.5 cm", "172 cm", "179 cm", "186 cm"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The mid-parental height is calculated as the **mean of the two parents' heights**, then **7 cm added for a boy** or **7 cm subtracted for a girl**. Here: (182 + 162) ÷ 2 = 172 cm; adding 7 cm for a male child gives **179 cm**. The centile closest to this value at age 18 on the child's growth chart is the mid-parental centile (MPC). Most children achieve a height within **two centile spaces** of the MPC; the further a child lies from it, the more likely a pathological cause. An alternative method plots both parents' heights on the parent height comparator printed on the growth chart.\n\n*(A, B)* These reflect subtraction of 7 cm (the calculation for a girl) or no correction applied to a lower value — both would underestimate a boy's target.\n\n*(C)* 172 cm is the *uncorrected* mid-parental height. Omitting the sex correction is the most common error; the 7 cm adjustment accounts for the average adult height difference between men and women, which arises because boys have a longer pre-pubertal childhood growth phase and a higher peak pubertal height velocity.\n\n*(E)* This exceeds the calculated target and corresponds to adding roughly twice the correction factor.", "objective": "Mid-parental height = mean of parental heights, **+7 cm for boys, −7 cm for girls**. A child's height should fall within two centile spaces of the resulting mid-parental centile.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-3", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "growth-puberty", "stem": "A female infant is born at 30 weeks' gestation. At 18 weeks of chronological age her weight is 4 kg, which plots well below the 0.4th centile on the standard growth chart, prompting concern about faltering growth.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate next step in interpreting her growth?", "options": ["Refer urgently for investigation of failure to thrive", "Plot her weight 10 weeks earlier on the chart to correct for gestational age", "Repeat the measurement using a different set of scales", "Reassure that preterm infants remain below the 0.4th centile permanently", "Calculate body mass index instead of using weight alone"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Infants born preterm must have their measurements **corrected for gestational age** before interpretation. This infant was born at 30 weeks, i.e. **10 weeks early**, so her plot is moved 10 weeks to the left on the chart. After correction, her weight lies between the **2nd and 9th centiles** — entirely within the normal range, and the apparent faltering growth disappears. Correction is conventionally continued for the **first year of life**, and in extremely preterm infants until **2 years of age**. Specific charts are also available for infants born below 32 weeks' gestation to allow closer monitoring.\n\n*(A)* Referral based on an uncorrected plot would generate unnecessary investigation and parental anxiety in an infant who is growing normally.\n\n*(C)* Measurement error is worth considering when a single value is discrepant from a previously established trend, but here the value is explicable entirely by gestational age and there is no suggestion of technical error.\n\n*(D)* This is untrue. Corrected plotting demonstrates normal growth; preterm infants are not expected to track permanently below the 0.4th centile.\n\n*(E)* BMI is used to assess whether a child is under- or overweight relative to height and does not address the gestational age correction, which is the specific issue here.", "objective": "Always correct growth measurements for gestational age in preterm infants — plot at actual age, then shift back by the number of weeks born early. Continue for the first year, or to 2 years if extremely preterm.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-4", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "growth-puberty", "stem": "A 4-month-old infant girl is brought for review. Her mother is anxious because the baby seems small. She was born at term weighing 3.2 kg and has been exclusively breastfed. She is alert, feeding well, and has met her developmental milestones.\n\nWhich of the following weights would be most consistent with normal growth at this age?", "options": ["4.0 kg", "4.8 kg", "6.2 kg", "8.0 kg", "9.6 kg"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Weight gain in the first year follows a predictable, decelerating pattern. In the **first 4 months** an infant gains approximately **¾ kg per month** (≈3 kg total); in the **second 4 months**, ½ kg per month (≈2 kg); and in the **third 4 months**, ¼ kg per month (≈1 kg) — a total gain of about 6 kg over the first year. Birth weight is therefore **doubled by around 4–5 months** and **tripled by 12 months**. For this infant: 3.2 kg + ~3 kg ≈ 6.2 kg, closely approximating a doubling of birth weight. Note also that an initial loss of **5–10% of birth weight** occurs in the first few days and is regained by **2 weeks**; loss persisting beyond this requires investigation.\n\n*(A, B)* These represent inadequate weight gain — less than half the expected increment — and would prompt assessment for faltering growth.\n\n*(D)* 8.0 kg represents a gain of nearly 5 kg in 4 months, well in excess of expected, and approximates the weight anticipated at closer to 9–12 months.\n\n*(E)* 9.6 kg is triple the birth weight, expected at approximately **12 months**, not 4 months.", "objective": "Birth weight doubles by ~4–5 months and triples by 12 months. Weight gain decelerates through infancy: ¾ kg/month, then ½ kg/month, then ¼ kg/month in successive 4-month blocks.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-5", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "growth-puberty", "stem": "A 3-month-old infant is noted to have a head that appears flattened at the back on the right, with the right ear displaced **anteriorly** relative to the left. Head circumference is tracking along the 50th centile. He sleeps supine and has mild hypotonia. The anterior fontanelle is open and soft.\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": ["Lambdoid craniosynostosis", "Sagittal craniosynostosis", "Occipital plagiocephaly", "Hydrocephalus", "Crouzon syndrome"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Occipital (positional) plagiocephaly produces a **parallelogram-shaped head** with occipital flattening, and the critical discriminating sign is that the **ipsilateral ear is displaced anteriorly**. All sutures remain open, head circumference grows normally, and the condition improves spontaneously as the infant becomes more mobile. Its incidence rose after the advice that infants sleep supine to reduce SIDS risk. It is also seen in infants with **hypotonia**, as here, who move their heads less. Management is positional and reassurance-based.\n\n*(A)* Lambdoid synostosis is the key mimic and is distinguished by the direction of ear displacement: the ear on the affected side is displaced **posteriorly**, and a fused, palpable ridge is present over the lambdoid suture. This is the single most useful bedside discriminator.\n\n*(B)* Sagittal synostosis, the most common form of localised craniosynostosis, produces a **long, narrow skull** (scaphocephaly) rather than asymmetrical occipital flattening.\n\n*(D)* Hydrocephalus causes head circumference to **cross centiles upward** with a tense fontanelle and signs of raised intracranial pressure; this infant's head circumference is tracking normally.\n\n*(E)* Crouzon syndrome is a syndromic craniosynostosis characterised by shallow orbits and **exophthalmos**, requiring craniofacial reconstruction to prevent visual loss.", "objective": "In occipital plagiocephaly the ear is displaced **anteriorly**, sutures are open, and the condition is positional and self-resolving. In lambdoid craniosynostosis the ear is displaced **posteriorly** and a fused suture is palpable.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-6", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "growth-puberty", "stem": "A 7-year-old girl is referred because of short stature. Her height is below the 0.4th centile and her growth velocity has been slow. She has a history of recurrent otitis media but is otherwise well. She has no dysmorphic features on cursory inspection.\n\nWhich investigation is most important to perform in this patient?", "options": ["Growth hormone provocation testing", "MRI of the brain", "Karyotype", "Limited skeletal survey", "Serum cortisol"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Turner syndrome (45,XO) must be excluded in **every girl with marked short stature**, because dysmorphic features may be subtle or entirely absent and the diagnosis is easily missed on clinical grounds alone. A history of **recurrent ear infections** is a recognised associated feature and a useful clue. Karyotype (or microarray) is therefore listed among the **standard investigations for all** children with short stature, specifically to check girls. Treatment is growth hormone injections followed by ethinyl estradiol for pubertal induction. The short stature in Turner syndrome is attributed to absence of one copy of the **SHOX** gene on the X chromosome (conversely, additional SHOX copies contribute to the tall stature of Klinefelter syndrome).\n\n*(A)* GH provocation testing is a specialist second-line investigation, considered only when the growth rate is slow **and IGF-1 is low**. IGF-1 is the appropriate screening test first.\n\n*(B)* MRI brain is indicated when there is headache or neurological signs, to exclude an intracranial tumour such as craniopharyngioma. Neither is present.\n\n*(D)* A limited skeletal survey is reserved for children with evidence of **skeletal disproportion**, to identify a skeletal dysplasia. Disproportion is assessed by comparing sitting height with leg length and is not described here.\n\n*(E)* Cortisol excess (Cushing syndrome) causes short stature with **relative overweight**; it is uncommon and not suggested by this presentation.", "objective": "Check a karyotype in **all girls with significant short stature** to exclude Turner syndrome, since dysmorphic features are often subtle. Recurrent otitis media is a supportive clue.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-7", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "growth-puberty", "stem": "A 12-year-old girl and her mother ask how much more she is likely to grow. She reached menarche 3 months ago. Her height is currently on the 50th centile.\n\nApproximately how much further height gain should be anticipated?", "options": ["1 cm", "5 cm", "12 cm", "20 cm", "Growth has already ceased"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Menarche occurs **late** in female puberty — approximately **2.5 years after its onset** — and signals that growth is drawing to a close. On average only about **5 cm of height gain remains** after menarche. This is a frequently examined and clinically useful figure when counselling families about final height, and it explains why interventions intended to influence adult height must be undertaken well before menarche.\n\nThe sequence in girls is: **breast bud (thelarche) first** — the key sign marking pubertal onset — followed soon after by pubic hair growth (adrenarche) and accelerated height velocity, with menarche last. Note the contrast with boys, in whom the first sign is **testicular enlargement above 4 mL** and height acceleration begins much later, at a testicular volume of **12–15 mL**, roughly 18 months after pubertal onset. Because boys have a longer pre-pubertal growth phase and a higher peak height velocity, their pubertal growth spurt is later and larger, accounting for greater adult male height.\n\n*(A)* This underestimates remaining growth; some growth reliably continues post-menarche.\n\n*(C, D)* These figures approximate the *total* pubertal growth spurt, not the residual growth after menarche. The pubertal phase contributes ~15% of final height overall, but most of it precedes menarche.\n\n*(E)* Growth does not cease abruptly at menarche.", "objective": "Menarche occurs ~2.5 years after pubertal onset, with only **~5 cm** of growth remaining. First sign of puberty: **breast bud in girls; testicular volume >4 mL in boys**.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-8", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "growth-puberty", "stem": "An 18-month-old girl is brought to clinic because of breast enlargement first noticed 4 months ago. The swelling is slightly asymmetrical and has fluctuated in size. She has no pubic or axillary hair, no acne, and no body odour. Her height and weight are tracking along the 50th centile with no recent acceleration.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": ["GnRH analogue therapy", "Reassurance and observation", "Cranial MRI", "Measurement of DHEA and 17-hydroxyprogesterone", "Pelvic ultrasound and bone age"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "This is **premature thelarche** — isolated breast development, typically affecting girls between **6 months and 2 years**, caused by transient activation of the hypothalamic–pituitary axis. The features that establish it as benign are all present: breast enlargement that may be **asymmetrical and fluctuates**, **absence of any other pubertal feature** (no pubic hair, acne, or body odour), and **no growth acceleration**. It rarely progresses beyond stage 3, is non-progressive and self-limiting, and **investigations are not usually required**. Reassurance with clinical observation is sufficient.\n\n*(A)* GnRH analogues are used in **gonadotrophin-dependent (central) precocious puberty**, where the full pubertal sequence is activated prematurely with accompanying growth acceleration. That is precisely what is absent here.\n\n*(C)* Cranial imaging is reserved for suspected central precocious puberty, particularly in boys, where an intracranial lesion is more likely to be responsible.\n\n*(D)* DHEA and 17-hydroxyprogesterone are indicated when **adrenarche** is accompanied by rapid growth or significant virilisation, to exclude excess adrenal androgen production. This girl has no pubic hair and no virilisation.\n\n*(E)* These investigations would be appropriate if precocious puberty were suspected; the isolated, non-progressive nature of the findings makes them unnecessary.", "objective": "Premature thelarche (6 months–2 years) is isolated, often asymmetrical, fluctuating breast development with **no other pubertal signs and no growth acceleration**. It is self-limiting and needs no investigation — distinguishing it from precocious puberty, in which growth accelerates and other pubertal features appear.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-9", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "growth-puberty", "stem": "A 9-year-old boy is referred with short stature. Measurement shows a total height well below the 0.4th centile. Sitting height is disproportionately preserved relative to leg length, and his legs appear short compared with his back. His mother is of average height and has no similar features.\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely category of underlying disorder?", "options": ["Growth hormone deficiency", "Skeletal dysplasia", "Emotional deprivation", "Coeliac disease", "Constitutional delay"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Short stature with **skeletal disproportion** points to a skeletal dysplasia such as achondroplasia. Disproportion is assessed by measuring **sitting height** (symphysis pubis to vertex) and comparing it with leg length (obtained by subtracting sitting height from total height); charts are available for interpretation. The pattern matters: in **skeletal dysplasias the legs are disproportionately short relative to the back**, whereas in **storage disorders the back is disproportionately short relative to the legs**. A limited skeletal survey is the appropriate investigation when disproportion is identified. Scoliosis is another skeletal cause of short stature.\n\n*(A)* GH deficiency produces **proportionate** short stature with relative overweight and markedly delayed bone age.\n\n*(C)* Emotional deprivation causes children to be short and underweight with delayed puberty, mediated by suppression of hypothalamic–pituitary function and reduced GH secretion. Growth improves in a nurturing environment. Body proportions remain normal.\n\n*(D)* Coeliac disease causes proportionate short stature with weight affected more than height; there is no skeletal disproportion.\n\n*(E)* Constitutional delay produces proportionate short stature with delayed bone age and delayed puberty, not disproportion.", "objective": "Short stature **with disproportion** indicates a skeletal dysplasia (legs short relative to back) or a storage disorder (back short relative to legs). Assess by comparing sitting height with leg length.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-10", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "nutrition", "stem": "A 13-month-old boy is brought to the emergency department after a generalised afebrile seizure. He was born at term and remains predominantly breastfed; weaning foods were offered at 7–8 months but he prefers the breast. He has only recently begun to sit without support and will not bear weight on standing. Examination shows frontal bossing, a wide anterior fontanelle, widened wrists, a palpable costochondral rosary, and Harrison sulci.\n\nWhich of the following sets of biochemical findings is most likely?", "options": ["Calcium low · Phosphate low · Alkaline phosphatase high · PTH high", "Calcium low · Phosphate high · Alkaline phosphatase low · PTH low", "Calcium high · Phosphate low · Alkaline phosphatase high · PTH low", "Calcium normal · Phosphate high · Alkaline phosphatase normal · PTH high", "Calcium high · Phosphate high · Alkaline phosphatase high · PTH high"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "This is nutritional (calciopenic) rickets from vitamin D deficiency — the classic picture in a prolonged-breastfed infant with delayed weaning and no supplementation. The biochemical cascade follows directly from the pathophysiology: **inadequate calcium absorption** triggers **secondary hyperparathyroidism (high PTH)**, which maintains serum calcium by demineralising bone and increasing **renal phosphate loss (low phosphate)**. Serum calcium is therefore **low or normal** depending on how well compensated the child is — when it falls, hypocalcaemic seizures, tetany, apnoea, stridor, or cardiomyopathy may occur, as here. Increased osteoblastic activity raises **alkaline phosphatase**, and 25-hydroxyvitamin D is low. Wrist or knee X-ray shows **cupping and fraying of the metaphysis with a widened growth plate**.\n\nNote the presentation timing: hypocalcaemic symptoms preceding overt rickets are more common **before 2 years of age and in adolescence**, when demand for calcium in rapidly growing bone is highest.\n\n*(B, D, E)* A high phosphate is inconsistent with PTH-driven renal phosphate wasting. A low ALP would argue against the increased bone turnover characteristic of rickets (and is instead seen in hypophosphatasia).\n\n*(C)* Hypercalcaemia with low PTH suggests vitamin D **toxicity** (hypervitaminosis D), which presents months after excessive dosing with anorexia, vomiting, constipation, polyuria, dehydration, hypotonia, and nephrocalcinosis.", "objective": "Nutritional rickets produces **low/normal calcium, LOW phosphate, HIGH alkaline phosphatase, HIGH PTH, and low 25-OH vitamin D**, with metaphyseal cupping and fraying on X-ray. Hypocalcaemic seizures may precede overt bony changes.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-11", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "nutrition", "stem": "A 9-month-old exclusively breastfed infant is being assessed at a routine visit. His mother asks about starting cow's milk as his main drink to reduce cost, and about introducing honey to sweeten his food.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate advice?", "options": ["Both cow's milk and honey may be introduced now", "Cow's milk may be introduced now; honey should be delayed until 12 months", "Cow's milk should be delayed until 12 months; honey may be introduced now", "Both cow's milk as a main drink and honey should be delayed until 12 months", "Both should be delayed until 2 years of age"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Two independent age thresholds converge at 12 months.\n\n**Pasteurised cow's milk should not be used as the main drink before 12 months** because its iron content is very low, and substituting it for breast milk or formula predisposes to **iron deficiency**. It may, however, be *incorporated into food* once weaning has begun — for example added to mashed potato — provided there is no cow's milk protein allergy. After 12 months whole cow's milk may replace formula, since solids should by then supply adequate iron. From **2 years** semi-skimmed milk may be introduced if growth and energy intake are satisfactory, and over **5 years** skimmed milk may be used.\n\n**Honey must not be given before 1 year of age because of the risk of infantile botulism.** This is an absolute weaning rule and a common exam point.\n\n*(A, B, C, E)* Each misapplies one or both thresholds. Note also the related weaning principles: solids are introduced at 4–6 months in most high-income settings; foods high in salt and sugar are avoided; and early introduction of common allergens such as **egg and peanut at around 4 months** in high-risk infants may *reduce* the risk of IgE-mediated food allergy.", "objective": "Cow's milk should not be the main drink before **12 months** (iron deficiency risk), and honey must be avoided before **12 months** (infantile botulism).", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-12", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "nutrition", "stem": "A mother of a healthy term neonate asks why breast milk protects her baby from infection when formula does not.\n\nWhich of the following is the principal protective component of human milk responsible for preventing bacterial invasion of the intestinal mucosa?", "options": ["Lactoferrin", "Secretory IgA", "Lysozyme", "Human milk oligosaccharides", "Maternal leucocytes"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "**Secretory IgA is the primary protective agent** in breast milk. It coats the intestinal mucosa and prevents bacteria from entering mucosal cells — a mechanical/immunological barrier at the precise site where enteric pathogens invade. This underpins the observed reduction in gastroenteritis, and explains why exclusive breastfeeding is lifesaving in low- and middle-income countries and forms one of the WHO's main strategies for reducing child mortality.\n\nThe other listed components are genuinely protective but secondary:\n\n*(A, C)* Lactoferrin and lysozyme are whey proteins that kill bacteria, viruses, and fungi, but they act by direct microbicidal effect rather than as the principal mucosal barrier.\n\n*(D)* Human milk oligosaccharides function as **prebiotics**, preventing pathogenic bacteria from attaching to mucosal surfaces and shaping a gut microbiome distinct from that of formula-fed infants.\n\n*(E)* Maternal white blood cells present in milk can kill micro-organisms but are a minor contributor.\n\nAlso worth knowing: **colostrum** (first 2–3 days, deep yellow, small volume) is especially rich in protein, immunoglobulin, zinc, and vitamin A, and primes the gut microbiome. Breastfeeding additionally reduces otitis media, lower respiratory tract infection, **necrotising enterocolitis in extremely preterm infants**, and SIDS.", "objective": "Secretory IgA is the primary anti-infective agent in breast milk, coating intestinal mucosa to block bacterial entry. HMOs act as prebiotics; lysozyme and lactoferrin are directly microbicidal.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-13", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "nutrition", "stem": "A term breastfed neonate is reviewed on day 3 of life. The mother is well and the baby is feeding 8 times daily with audible swallowing and 6 wet nappies per day. The infant's birthweight was 3.4 kg and today's weight is 3.1 kg.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate action?", "options": ["Reassure and continue exclusive breastfeeding", "Supplement with formula after each breastfeed", "Admit for investigation of dehydration", "Give water supplements between feeds", "Perform a septic screen"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "This infant has lost 8.8% of birthweight, which lies within the expected physiological range. Healthy term babies commonly lose **7%–10% of birthweight** in the first days of life owing to fluid shifts, and **regain birthweight by 14 days**. The reassuring features here are all present: frequent feeding, audible and visible swallowing, and **6 or more wet nappies daily**. In the first few days milk volume is low, but **water or formula supplements are not required while the breast milk supply is becoming established** — supplementation at this point undermines establishment of lactation by reducing the suckling stimulus.\n\nNote that milk production is regulated locally by **feedback inhibitor of lactation (FIL)**, a polypeptide in breast milk: as it accumulates in a full breast it suppresses further secretion, so **regular emptying of the breast is required to sustain supply**. Mothers typically feel the milk \"coming in\" at 2–3 days.\n\n*(B, D)* Supplementation is not indicated and is counterproductive. Newborns of mothers planning to breastfeed should not receive formula unless unavoidable.\n\n*(C, E)* There are no features of dehydration or sepsis; output and feeding behaviour are normal.\n\n**The threshold that does require assessment:** weight loss exceeding **10%**, or failure to regain birthweight by **14 days**.", "objective": "A 7–10% weight loss in the first days of life is physiological, with birthweight regained by 14 days. Loss >10% or failure to regain by 14 days requires assessment for an underlying medical problem.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-14", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "nutrition", "stem": "A 15-month-old girl is referred because her weight has fallen from the 50th centile at 6 months to just below the 9th centile. She is described as a fussy eater who grazes on snacks and drinks large volumes of diluted fruit cordial. She is otherwise well, developmentally normal, with no vomiting, diarrhoea, or respiratory symptoms. Examination is unremarkable.\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely cause of her faltering growth?", "options": ["Coeliac disease", "Cow's milk protein allergy", "Inadequate energy intake", "Chronic kidney disease", "Cystic fibrosis"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "In the great majority of children with faltering growth **no underlying pathology is found**, and the problem is **inadequate food intake**. An organic cause accounts for only about **5%** of cases, and when present there are almost always **symptoms or signs pointing to the underlying disease** — which are conspicuously absent here. This child's history instead reveals classic dietary and behavioural contributors: irregular grazing rather than structured meals, and excessive intake of diluted cordial, which displaces energy-dense food.\n\nManagement is largely in primary care: three meals and two snacks daily, increasing the energy density of foods (adding cheese, butter, or cream), limiting milk intake, avoiding excessive fruit juice and cordial, establishing regular mealtimes limited to 30 minutes, praising eating and ignoring refusal, and **never force feeding**. The key outcome measure is weight gain with a rise up the centiles, which typically begins **4–8 weeks after intervention**.\n\n*(A)* Coeliac disease is an important organic cause and may present with slow growth alone, but IgA tTG serology would be checked only when pathology is suspected — the history here explains the picture.\n\n*(B)* Cow's milk protein allergy causes faltering growth through vomiting and inadequate retention; no such symptoms are described.\n\n*(D, E)* Chronic kidney disease and cystic fibrosis increase requirements or cause malabsorption, but both would be expected to produce additional features (renal symptoms, respiratory symptoms, steatorrhoea, positive sweat test).", "objective": "Faltering growth is usually due to **inadequate intake**; organic disease accounts for only ~5% and is nearly always accompanied by other symptoms and signs. Expect weight gain 4–8 weeks after dietary and behavioural intervention.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-15", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "nutrition", "stem": "A 4-week-old infant with confirmed cow's milk protein allergy requires a formula. His mother asks whether soya-based formula can be used, as it is cheaper and available locally.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate response?", "options": ["Soya formula is first-line for cow's milk protein allergy at any age", "Soya formula is not recommended under 6 months and is usually avoided in the first year", "Soya formula should be used only if extensively hydrolysed formula fails", "Soya formula is contraindicated because it lacks essential amino acids", "Soya formula may be used but requires calcium supplementation"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Soya-based formula was historically used for cow's milk protein allergy but is now largely avoided. It is **not recommended under 6 months** and is usually avoided **throughout the first year** for two reasons: it contains **phytoestrogens** (plant substances mimicking endogenous oestrogens), and there is a substantial risk of **concurrent soy protein allergy** in infants already allergic to cow's milk protein. After the first year, ordinary soya milk may be used.\n\nThe appropriate choices for cow's milk protein allergy are **extensively hydrolysed formula** (protein broken down to reduce allergenicity) or, where that is not tolerated, **amino acid formula** (protein completely replaced by free amino acids).\n\nOther specialised formulas worth recognising:\n- **Preterm formula** — increased energy, sodium, calcium, and phosphate to match preterm requirements\n- **MCT formula** — for fat malabsorption; medium-chain triglycerides are absorbed directly into the small intestine **without requiring pancreatic enzymes or bile salts**\n- **Anti-reflux (pre-thickened) formula** — contains rice starch or carob bean gum, which thickens as gastric pH falls\n\n*(A, C)* These misstate current practice; soya is not first-line and is avoided by age rather than by treatment sequence.\n\n*(D)* Soya formulas are nutritionally complete; the concern is phytoestrogens and cross-allergy, not amino acid deficiency.\n\n*(E)* Calcium supplementation is not the governing issue.", "objective": "For cow's milk protein allergy use **extensively hydrolysed or amino acid formula**. Soya formula is avoided under 6 months and generally in the first year because of phytoestrogens and cross-reactive soy allergy.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-16", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "nutrition", "stem": "A term newborn is delivered and the parents decline the routine intramuscular injection offered shortly after birth, stating they wish everything to be natural and that the baby will be exclusively breastfed.\n\nDeficiency of which vitamin is this infant at greatest immediate risk of, and with what consequence?", "options": ["Vitamin A — xerophthalmia", "Vitamin D — rickets", "Vitamin E — haemolytic anaemia", "Vitamin K — bleeding", "Vitamin B12 — megaloblastic anaemia"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Newborn infants are **physiologically vitamin K deficient**, and **breast milk contains insufficient vitamin K** to prevent haemorrhagic disease of the newborn (vitamin K deficient bleeding). Vitamin K is therefore given **intramuscularly (occasionally orally) at birth** to all infants. Deficiency causes coagulation abnormalities leading to bruising and bleeding, which may be catastrophic if intracranial. Dietary sources are green leafy vegetables (richest), other vegetables, fruits, dairy, vegetable oils, meats, and cereals, and it is also synthesised by intestinal bacteria — a source not yet established in the newborn gut.\n\n*(A)* Vitamin A deficiency causes **xerophthalmia** (dryness of conjunctiva and cornea), **night blindness**, and increased susceptibility to infection — especially **measles**. It is a problem of low-income settings and fat malabsorption, not the immediate newborn period.\n\n*(B)* Vitamin D deficiency is a genuine concern in exclusively breastfed infants, and all infants and children up to 5 years should receive supplements — but rickets develops over months, not immediately, and vitamin D is given orally rather than by the injection offered at birth.\n\n*(C)* Vitamin E deficiency causes haemolytic anaemia, retinopathy, and progressive neuropathy, particularly in **preterm** infants and fat malabsorption.\n\n*(E)* Vitamin B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anaemia and is seen in unsupplemented vegan diets or after small intestinal resection.", "objective": "All newborns require **vitamin K at birth** because breast milk contains insufficient vitamin K to prevent haemorrhagic disease of the newborn. Vitamin D supplementation is separately recommended for all children up to 5 years.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-17", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "nutrition", "stem": "A 2-month-old infant is exclusively breastfed. Comparing the composition of mature breast milk with unmodified cow's milk, which of the following differences correctly explains why unmodified cow's milk is unsuitable for young infants?", "options": ["Cow's milk has a lower protein content, causing inadequate growth", "Cow's milk has a casein:whey ratio of 40:60, which is poorly digested", "Cow's milk has a substantially higher sodium and protein content, imposing a high renal solute load", "Cow's milk contains more iron, causing gastrointestinal irritation", "Cow's milk has a higher carbohydrate content, causing osmotic diarrhoea"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Unmodified cow's milk differs from mature breast milk in ways that make it unsuitable for young infants. Per 100 mL, protein is roughly **3.4 g in cow's milk versus 1.0 g** in breast milk, and sodium is approximately **1.9 mmol versus 0.65 mmol** — nearly threefold higher for each. Calcium and phosphorus are similarly several-fold higher. This imposes a **high renal solute load** on immature kidneys. Infant formula is modified cow's milk in which these constituents are brought closer to the breast milk profile.\n\n*(A)* The reverse is true — cow's milk protein is higher, not lower.\n\n*(B)* The casein:whey ratio of cow's milk is approximately **80:20**; it is **breast milk** that is 40:60. Formulas are manufactured across the range 40:60 to 80:20.\n\n*(D)* Cow's milk contains substantially **less** iron than breast milk (≈0.36 vs 12 µmol), which is precisely why using it as the main drink before 12 months predisposes to iron deficiency.\n\n*(E)* Carbohydrate is **lower** in cow's milk (≈4.6 g vs 7.0 g).", "objective": "Compared with breast milk, cow's milk has ~3× the protein and sodium, higher calcium and phosphate, much **lower iron**, lower carbohydrate, and a casein:whey ratio of **80:20** (breast milk 40:60).", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-18", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "nutrition", "stem": "A 2-year-old child of South Asian descent living in a northern latitude is diagnosed with nutritional rickets. He was exclusively breastfed into late infancy without vitamin supplementation.\n\nWhich of the following is the correct treatment and prevention regimen?", "options": ["Treat with 400 IU daily; prevent with 400 IU daily from birth", "Treat with 4000–6000 IU daily; after healing continue 400 IU daily", "Treat with 800 IU daily; prevent with 800 IU daily in all infants", "Treat with 600,000 IU daily for one week; then stop", "No treatment required; sunlight exposure alone is sufficient"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Nutritional rickets is treated with an **oral daily dose of 4000–6000 IU of vitamin D**, together with ensuring adequate dietary calcium. Where compliance is poor, a **single large dose of 600,000 IU** (oral or intramuscular) may be given. **After healing, the child returns to a daily maintenance dose of 400 IU.**\n\nHealing is monitored radiologically — disappearance of **cupping and broadening** on wrist and ankle X-ray. Bone healing begins within 2–4 weeks, though complete resolution of bony deformity may take **several years**.\n\nFor prevention, the thresholds differ by gestation: **term infants receive 400 IU from 3 months**, whereas **preterm infants receive 800 IU from day one**, reflecting their lower mineral stores and higher requirements.\n\nThis child carries several classic risk factors: **dark skin**, residence at a **northern latitude**, and **prolonged exclusive breastfeeding into late infancy** without supplementation. Nutritional rickets has re-emerged in high-income countries specifically in this group.\n\n*(A, C)* These confuse maintenance/prevention doses with treatment doses.\n\n*(D)* 600,000 IU is a single rescue dose for non-compliance, not a daily regimen — repeated dosing at this level risks hypervitaminosis D and hypercalcaemia.\n\n*(E)* Established rickets requires pharmacological replacement; sunlight advice is part of preventing recurrence, not treatment.", "objective": "Treat nutritional rickets with **4000–6000 IU/day** (or a single 600,000 IU dose if non-compliant), then maintain on **400 IU/day**. Prevention: **400 IU from 3 months in term infants; 800 IU from day 1 in preterm infants**.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-19", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "gastroenterology", "stem": "A 5-week-old boy is brought to the emergency department with a 10-day history of vomiting after feeds, which has become increasingly forceful and now shoots across the room. The vomit contains milk but no bile. He appears hungry and feeds eagerly immediately after vomiting. He is the first child of healthy parents; his mother had similar symptoms as an infant. He has lost 200 g since last week.\n\nWhich of the following blood gas and electrolyte patterns is most likely?", "options": ["Metabolic acidosis with hyperkalaemia and hyperchloraemia", "Hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis", "Respiratory alkalosis with hypernatraemia", "Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis with hypokalaemia", "Metabolic alkalosis with hyperchloraemia and hypernatraemia"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis produces a **hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis**, and hyponatraemia may coexist. The mechanism follows directly from the site of obstruction: gastric outlet obstruction means the infant vomits **gastric contents only** — rich in hydrogen and chloride ions — without loss of alkaline duodenal secretions. Loss of H⁺ and Cl⁻ generates the alkalosis and hypochloraemia; secondary hyperaldosteronism from volume depletion drives renal potassium loss.\n\nEvery other feature here is classic: presentation at **2–8 weeks of age irrespective of gestational age**, **non-bilious** vomiting increasing in frequency and force until projectile, a hungry infant who **feeds normally after vomiting** until dehydration supervenes, weight loss with delayed presentation, male predominance (**4:1**), **first-born**, and a family history especially on the **maternal** side.\n\nDiagnosis is by **ultrasound** (now standard), or by test feed with palpation of the olive-shaped pyloric mass in the right upper quadrant — the stomach often needs emptying by nasogastric tube first, and visible gastric peristalsis may be seen moving left to right.\n\n*(A, D)* Acidosis would be expected with diarrhoeal losses of bicarbonate, not gastric losses.\n\n*(C, E)* Hyperchloraemia and hypernatraemia are the opposite of the expected pattern.", "objective": "Pyloric stenosis (2–8 weeks, male, first-born, non-bilious projectile vomiting, hungry infant) causes **hypochloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis**. Correct fluid and electrolyte abnormalities before surgery.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-20", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "gastroenterology", "stem": "The infant described above is confirmed to have pyloric stenosis on ultrasound. He is 8% dehydrated with a chloride of 88 mmol/L and potassium of 3.0 mmol/L.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": ["Immediate pyloromyotomy", "Intravenous fluid rehydration and electrolyte correction before surgery", "Nasogastric feeding with thickened formula", "Trial of intravenous omeprazole", "Rectal air insufflation"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Pyloric stenosis is **not a surgical emergency** — it is a medical emergency followed by elective surgery. Pyloromyotomy **can only be performed safely after acid–base and electrolyte imbalances have been corrected**, which may take **more than 24 hours** of intravenous rehydration. Operating on an alkalotic, hypokalaemic, dehydrated infant risks postoperative apnoea (alkalosis depresses respiratory drive) and arrhythmia. This is a frequently examined principle and the single most common error in managing these infants.\n\nOnce corrected, **pyloromyotomy** involves dividing the hypertrophied muscle **down to but not including the mucosa**, performed open via a periumbilical incision or laparoscopically. Feeding usually resumes within a few hours and discharge follows within a couple of days.\n\n*(A)* Immediate surgery before correction is unsafe, as above.\n\n*(C, D)* Thickened feeds and acid suppression are treatments for gastro-oesophageal reflux, a different condition. Reflux causes recurrent regurgitation in a **thriving, well** infant during the first year, without the projectile pattern, electrolyte disturbance, or weight loss seen here.\n\n*(E)* Rectal air insufflation is the reduction technique for **intussusception**.", "objective": "In pyloric stenosis, rehydrate and correct the hypochloraemic hypokalaemic alkalosis first — surgery is deferred until biochemistry is normal, often >24 hours.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-21", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "gastroenterology", "stem": "An 8-month-old boy presents with a 12-hour history of episodes of screaming during which he draws his legs up and becomes pale around the mouth. Episodes last a few minutes and recur every 15–20 minutes, with apparent recovery in between, though he is becoming progressively more lethargic. He has vomited three times. On examination he is tachycardic with a capillary refill time of 4 seconds and a sausage-shaped mass is palpable in the upper abdomen.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate sequence of management?", "options": ["Immediate rectal air insufflation, then fluid resuscitation", "Fluid resuscitation, then rectal air insufflation under surgical supervision", "Immediate laparotomy without imaging", "Barium enema for diagnosis, then observation", "Nasogastric decompression and oral rehydration solution"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "This is intussusception, and the order of interventions matters. **Fluid resuscitation comes first** because pooling of fluid within the gut causes hypovolaemic shock — as evidenced here by tachycardia and a capillary refill time of 4 seconds. **Only after resuscitation** is reduction attempted by **rectal air insufflation**, performed by a radiologist **under the supervision of a paediatric surgeon** in case of failure or perforation. Success rate is approximately **75%**; the remaining 25% require operative reduction. Recurrence occurs in **fewer than 5%**, more often after hydrostatic reduction.\n\nThe clinical picture is characteristic: peak incidence **3 months to 2 years**, paroxysmal severe colicky pain with **pallor and drawing up of the legs**, recovery between episodes with progressive lethargy, vomiting, and a **sausage-shaped abdominal mass**. **Redcurrant jelly stool** (blood-stained mucus) is characteristic but occurs **late** and may first appear only after rectal examination — its absence does not exclude the diagnosis. Ultrasound shows the **target/doughnut sign**.\n\nThe urgency arises because stretching and constriction of the mesentery causes **venous obstruction**, leading to mucosal engorgement and bleeding, fluid loss, and ultimately perforation, peritonitis, and gut necrosis.\n\n*(A)* Reversing the order risks attempting reduction in a shocked infant.\n\n*(C)* Laparotomy is reserved for failed reduction or peritonitis.\n\n*(D)* Observation is inappropriate for a time-critical vascular compromise.\n\n*(E)* Oral rehydration is inadequate for shock and inappropriate with bowel obstruction.", "objective": "In intussusception: resuscitate first, then attempt **rectal air insufflation** (75% success) under surgical supervision. Air insufflation is contraindicated if peritonitis is present — proceed to surgery.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-22", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "gastroenterology", "stem": "A 3-day-old term infant develops dark green vomiting. He had been feeding well. Abdominal examination shows minimal distension and no tenderness. He is haemodynamically stable.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate next investigation?", "options": ["Abdominal ultrasound", "Urgent upper gastrointestinal contrast study", "Plain abdominal radiograph and observation", "Suction rectal biopsy", "Technetium scan"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "**Any child with dark green (bile-stained) vomiting requires an urgent upper gastrointestinal contrast study to assess intestinal rotation**, unless there are signs of vascular compromise, in which case urgent laparotomy takes precedence. Bilious vomiting in a neonate is malrotation with volvulus until proven otherwise.\n\nThe anatomical basis: in malrotation the intestine fails to rotate and fix normally, leaving the small intestine predominantly on the right with the caecum in the right upper quadrant. **Ladd bands** tether the caecum to the right upper quadrant and compress the duodenum, causing obstruction; the poorly fixed mesentery allows the gut to twist, producing **volvulus**. Volvulus compromises the **superior mesenteric artery**, and unless corrected leads to infarction of the small intestine and proximal large bowel — hence the surgical emergency.\n\nAt operation the volvulus is untwisted, the duodenum mobilised, the bowel placed in the non-rotated position (duodenojejunal flexure right, caecum and appendix left), and the **appendix removed** to avoid future diagnostic confusion.\n\nNote the general rule: the presence of **green bile in vomit is an emergency**, indicating obstruction distal to the ampulla of Vater with reversed bile flow. Parents may loosely use \"bile\" to mean clear acidic stomach contents — this must be clarified.\n\n*(A)* Ultrasound is the investigation for pyloric stenosis (non-bilious vomiting) and supports intussusception, not malrotation.\n\n*(C)* Observation of bilious vomiting risks bowel infarction.\n\n*(D)* Suction rectal biopsy diagnoses Hirschsprung disease, which presents with failure to pass meconium and abdominal distension.\n\n*(E)* Technetium scanning identifies ectopic gastric mucosa in Meckel diverticulum.", "objective": "Bilious vomiting in a neonate is malrotation with volvulus until excluded — obtain an **urgent upper GI contrast study**, or proceed directly to laparotomy if there is vascular compromise.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-23", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "gastroenterology", "stem": "A 3-year-old girl has had watery diarrhoea and vomiting for 3 days. She has become drowsy. Her skin turgor is difficult to assess and her fontanelle is closed. She feels \"doughy\" on palpation. Serum sodium is 158 mmol/L.\n\nWhich of the following is the most important principle in her fluid management?", "options": ["Rapid correction of sodium with 0.45% saline over 6 hours", "Correction of plasma sodium slowly, over at least 48 hours, at less than 0.5 mmol/L per hour", "Fluid restriction to two-thirds maintenance", "Immediate hypertonic saline to prevent cerebral oedema", "Oral rehydration solution is contraindicated in hypernatraemia"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "In **hypernatraemic dehydration**, the reduction in plasma sodium must be **slow — over at least 48 hours, aiming for less than 0.5 mmol/L per hour** — using 0.9% saline or 0.9% saline with 5% glucose, with regular measurement of plasma sodium. Rapid correction causes water to shift into cerebral cells, producing **seizures and cerebral oedema**.\n\nThe pathophysiology explains both the danger and the diagnostic difficulty. Water loss exceeds sodium loss (from high insensible losses with fever or a hot dry environment, or profuse low-sodium diarrhoea), so extracellular fluid becomes hypertonic and water moves **out of cells into the extracellular space**. Consequently the usual peripheral signs of dehydration — sunken fontanelle, reduced tissue elasticity, sunken eyes — are **less marked per unit of fluid lost**, making this form of dehydration **easy to underestimate clinically**, particularly in obese infants. Meanwhile water is drawn out of the brain, and cerebral shrinkage within a rigid skull produces irritability and abnormal neurological signs.\n\nContrast with **hyponatraemic dehydration**, which occurs when children with diarrhoea drink large volumes of water or hypotonic fluids: water shifts *into* cells, increasing brain volume and risking seizures, while marked extracellular depletion produces **early, exaggerated** peripheral signs and greater susceptibility to shock.\n\n*(A, D)* Both risk rapid osmotic shifts and cerebral oedema.\n\n*(C)* Two-thirds maintenance is used where SIADH is a risk, such as pneumonia.\n\n*(E)* ORS remains the mainstay of treatment for clinical dehydration; intravenous fluids are reserved for shock, deterioration, or persistent vomiting.", "objective": "Correct hypernatraemic dehydration **slowly over ≥48 hours at <0.5 mmol/L/hour**. Clinical signs underestimate the deficit because extracellular volume is relatively preserved.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-24", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "gastroenterology", "stem": "An 11 kg toddler is assessed as 10% dehydrated following gastroenteritis and meets clinical criteria for shock. She receives a 20 mL/kg bolus of 0.9% sodium chloride with good effect.\n\nUsing standard maintenance formulae, what is her remaining fluid requirement for the first 24 hours?", "options": ["1050 mL", "1100 mL", "1930 mL", "2150 mL", "2370 mL"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The calculation has three components:\n\n**Deficit:** 10% of 11 kg = 1100 mL\n**Maintenance** (the 100/50/20 rule): 100 mL/kg for the first 10 kg = 1000 mL; 50 mL/kg for the next 10 kg — here only 1 kg — = 50 mL. Total maintenance = **1050 mL**\n**Subtotal:** 1100 + 1050 = 2150 mL\n**Less the resuscitation bolus already given:** 20 mL/kg × 11 = 220 mL\n**Remaining requirement:** 2150 − 220 = **1930 mL**, i.e. approximately 80 mL/hour.\n\nFluid of choice for intravenous maintenance is **0.9% NaCl with 5% dextrose**, with consideration of 20 mmol KCl per 500 mL bag according to serum levels.\n\nFluids are then adjusted for the clinical situation: reduced to **two-thirds maintenance** where SIADH is a risk (e.g. pneumonia); **increased** for ongoing losses such as profuse diarrhoea or the diuretic phase after acute tubular necrosis; **reduced** to account for oral intake; and the replacement period **extended to 48 hours** in hypernatraemic dehydration or diabetic ketoacidosis to avoid rapid osmolar shifts.\n\n*(A)* 1050 mL is maintenance alone. *(B)* 1100 mL is the deficit alone. *(D)* 2150 mL omits subtraction of the bolus. *(E)* Does not correspond to the standard calculation.", "objective": "Total fluid = deficit + maintenance − resuscitation boluses given. Maintenance follows **100 mL/kg (first 10 kg) + 50 mL/kg (second 10 kg) + 20 mL/kg thereafter** per 24 hours.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-25", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "gastroenterology", "stem": "A 20-month-old boy is referred with poor growth since 12 months of age. His parents report he is irritable and grumpy and passes three or four foul-smelling stools daily that are difficult to flush. Examination shows wasting of the buttocks and a distended abdomen. His weight has fallen from the 50th to below the 2nd centile.\n\nWhich of the following investigations is most appropriate initially?", "options": ["Sweat test", "IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase antibodies with total IgA", "Stool microscopy and culture", "Hydrogen breath test", "Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy with duodenal biopsy"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "This is the **classical presentation of coeliac disease** — a profound malabsorptive syndrome at **8–24 months of age**, appearing after the introduction of wheat-containing weaning foods, with faltering growth, abdominal distension, **buttock wasting**, abnormal stools, and irritability. Note the description of the malabsorption stool: difficult to flush and foul-smelling.\n\nSerology is the appropriate first investigation: **IgA anti-tTG** and **IgA EMA** are highly sensitive and specific. **Total IgA must be measured alongside**, because IgA deficiency produces false-negative results.\n\nCoeliac disease is an immune-mediated systemic disorder triggered by gluten and related prolamines in **wheat, barley, and rye**, in individuals carrying **HLA DQ2 or DQ8**. Prevalence is approximately **1 in 100**, though the classical presentation occurs in only about 1 in 3000 — most children now present later and more subtly with anaemia, abdominal pain, short stature, delayed puberty, or on screening.\n\n**Screen high-risk groups:** type 1 diabetes mellitus, autoimmune thyroid disease, Down syndrome, and first-degree relatives.\n\n*(E)* Duodenal biopsy showing villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and intraepithelial lymphocytosis confirms the diagnosis, **but may be omitted** where a symptomatic child has markedly raised IgA anti-tTG **and** positive IgA EMA. Serology comes first regardless.\n\n*(A)* Cystic fibrosis also causes malabsorption and faltering growth, but would typically feature respiratory symptoms; it is a reasonable later consideration.\n\n*(C)* Infective causes do not explain months of progressive faltering growth.\n\n*(D)* Hydrogen breath testing assesses lactose malabsorption, which would not cause buttock wasting and this degree of growth failure.", "objective": "Investigate suspected coeliac disease with **IgA anti-tTG plus total IgA**. Biopsy confirms but may be omitted if serology is markedly positive in a symptomatic child. Treatment is a **lifelong gluten-free diet**; non-adherence risks osteopenia and small bowel lymphoma.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-26", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "gastroenterology", "stem": "A 3-year-old girl is referred with loose stools occurring several times daily for 4 months. The stools vary in consistency and often contain recognisable pieces of vegetables. She is thriving, with weight and height tracking along the 50th centile, and is developmentally normal. She drinks approximately one litre of apple juice daily.\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": ["Coeliac disease", "Cystic fibrosis", "Chronic non-specific diarrhoea of childhood", "Inflammatory bowel disease", "Giardiasis"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Chronic non-specific diarrhoea (formerly \"toddler diarrhoea\") is the **most common cause of persistent loose stools in preschool children**. The defining combination is **loose stools of varying consistency containing undigested vegetables in a child who is well and thriving**. Growth is normal — this is the single most important discriminator from organic disease.\n\nTwo contributory factors should be sought and addressed: **excessive fruit juice ingestion, especially apple juice** (as here — one litre daily is substantial), and undiagnosed coeliac disease in a proportion of children. Occasionally the cause is temporary cow's milk allergy following gastroenteritis, when a trial of a cow's milk protein-free diet may help.\n\nOnce underlying causes are excluded, the loose stools are attributed to **gut dysmotility with fast transit** — a form of irritable bowel syndrome — and **it almost always improves with age**.\n\n*(A)* Coeliac disease characteristically causes faltering growth; this child is thriving. It nonetheless remains worth excluding, as it can masquerade as chronic non-specific diarrhoea.\n\n*(B)* Cystic fibrosis produces steatorrhoea with malabsorption, poor growth, and respiratory disease.\n\n*(D)* Inflammatory bowel disease would be expected to cause weight loss, blood in stool, and systemic features. Note that although IBD incidence is rising in children, bowel cancer remains extremely rare in this age group.\n\n*(E)* Giardia can cause chronic diarrhoea and malabsorption but would generally affect growth over 4 months.", "objective": "In an otherwise well, thriving toddler with **undigested vegetables in loose stools**, consider chronic non-specific diarrhoea. Reduce fruit juice intake, exclude coeliac disease, and reassure — it resolves with age.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-27", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "gastroenterology", "stem": "A 4-year-old boy has a 6-month history of passing hard stools twice weekly with pain on defecation. Over the past 6 weeks his underwear has been soiled with loose stool several times daily, which his mother interprets as diarrhoea. Examination shows a well child with normal growth, a soft abdomen with a palpable mass in the left lower quadrant, and a normal perianal appearance.\n\nWhich of the following best explains the soiling, and what is the first-line treatment?", "options": ["Infective diarrhoea; oral rehydration solution", "Overflow incontinence from faecal impaction; macrogol (polyethylene glycol) in escalating dose", "Encopresis of behavioural origin; behavioural therapy alone", "Hirschsprung disease; suction rectal biopsy then surgery", "Irritable bowel syndrome; dietary fibre supplementation"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The soiling is **overflow incontinence**, not diarrhoea. Long-standing constipation causes **rectal overdistension with loss of the urge to defecate**; the contractions of a full rectum inhibit the internal sphincter, and soft or watery stool leaks around a large impacted mass. Recognising this prevents the common error of treating the child for diarrhoea.\n\n**Faecal impaction** is diagnosed by the combination of severe symptoms, overflow soiling, and a palpable faecal mass — exactly this presentation.\n\nTreatment begins with **disimpaction using macrogol laxatives (polyethylene glycol) in an escalating dose over 1–2 weeks**, adding a **stimulant laxative** (senna or sodium picosulphate) if unsuccessful, or substituting an osmotic laxative such as lactulose if macrogol is not tolerated. Maintenance then continues with polyethylene glycol, reduced gradually over months. Key principle: **laxatives are safe even long-term, and underuse is the commonest reason for treatment failure.**\n\nAdjuncts include scheduled toileting after meals (exploiting the gastrocolic reflex), star charts, and family support. **Dietary intervention alone has no benefit**, and **extra dietary fibre should be avoided** as it may worsen stool bulk and difficulty.\n\n*(D)* Hirschsprung disease is excluded here by normal growth and, critically, by the absence of **red flags**: failure to pass meconium within 24 hours of birth, constipation from birth, abdominal distension with vomiting, ribbon stools, or abnormal lower limb neurology. **95% of childhood constipation is idiopathic.**\n\n*(E)* Fibre supplementation is specifically discouraged.", "objective": "Soiling in chronic constipation is **overflow incontinence from impaction**, not diarrhoea. Treat with escalating **macrogol**, then maintenance; avoid extra fibre; underdosing is the main cause of failure.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-28", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "gastroenterology", "stem": "A term neonate has not passed meconium by 30 hours of age. He has abdominal distension and has begun to vomit bile-stained fluid. On rectal examination the examining finger meets a narrowed segment, and its withdrawal releases a gush of liquid stool and flatus with temporary improvement in distension.\n\nWhich of the following is the definitive diagnostic investigation?", "options": ["Upper gastrointestinal contrast study", "Anorectal manometry", "Suction rectal biopsy", "Barium enema", "Abdominal ultrasound"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "**Suction rectal biopsy** is the definitive investigation for Hirschsprung disease, demonstrating **absence of ganglion cells** in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses together with **large acetylcholinesterase-positive nerve trunks**.\n\nThe clinical picture is characteristic: failure to pass meconium within the **first 24 hours of life**, abdominal distension, and later bile-stained vomiting. The rectal examination finding described — a narrowed segment with a gush of stool and flatus on withdrawal of the finger, temporarily relieving the obstruction — is classic and, importantly, **can delay diagnosis** by producing apparent improvement.\n\nThe aganglionic segment extends proximally from the rectum for a variable distance, ending in a dilated, normally innervated colon. In **75%** of cases the lesion is confined to the **rectosigmoid**; in **10%** the entire colon is involved. Infants may occasionally present with life-threatening **Hirschsprung enterocolitis** in the first weeks of life. Later childhood presentation is with **profound chronic constipation with abdominal distension but usually without soiling** — a useful contrast with idiopathic constipation, in which overflow soiling is common — often with growth faltering.\n\n*(B, D)* Anorectal manometry and barium studies may help the surgeon estimate the length of the aganglionic segment but are **unreliable for diagnosis**.\n\n*(A)* Upper GI contrast assesses malrotation.\n\n*(E)* Ultrasound has no diagnostic role here.\n\nManagement is surgical, usually an initial colostomy followed by anastomosis of normally innervated bowel to the anus.", "objective": "Hirschsprung disease — failure to pass meconium within 24 hours, distension, bilious vomiting — is diagnosed by **suction rectal biopsy** showing absent ganglion cells. 75% are rectosigmoid.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-29", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "gastroenterology", "stem": "A 6-year-old boy of Mediterranean descent has a 2-year history of recurrent episodes of fever lasting 1–2 days, accompanied by severe abdominal pain with peritonism, and occasionally pleuritic chest pain and an erysipelas-like rash on the lower leg. He has undergone one negative laparotomy for suspected appendicitis. Between attacks he is entirely well. Attacks occur irregularly, from weekly to a few times yearly.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate long-term management?", "options": ["Lifelong prophylactic colchicine", "Long-term corticosteroids", "Prophylactic antibiotics", "Gluten-free diet", "Proton pump inhibitor"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "This is familial Mediterranean fever (FMF), and **lifelong prophylactic colchicine is the treatment of choice**. It both reduces the frequency of acute attacks and — critically — **prevents the development of AA amyloidosis**, the most devastating complication, which predominantly affects the **kidneys** but may involve other organs.\n\nFMF is a hereditary autoinflammatory disease caused by **gain-of-function mutations in the MEFV gene**, which encodes pyrin and is associated with release of **interleukin-1β**, driving inflammation. It is prevalent in populations surrounding the Mediterranean.\n\nThe clinical pattern is diagnostic: recurrent, **sporadic, self-limited** episodes of fever with **peritonitis, pleuritis, arthritis, and/or erysipelas-like erythema**. A typical attack lasts **0.5–3 days**, with patients **mostly asymptomatic between attacks**. Despite the older term \"periodic disease,\" attacks are **random rather than cyclical** — a point worth remembering. The peritonitic presentation frequently leads to negative laparotomy, as here.\n\n*(B)* Corticosteroids are not the maintenance treatment and carry unacceptable long-term toxicity in children.\n\n*(C)* The episodes are autoinflammatory, not infective.\n\n*(D)* A gluten-free diet treats coeliac disease.\n\n*(E)* PPIs treat acid-related disease; they have no role in FMF.", "objective": "Familial Mediterranean fever (MEFV mutation) causes short, self-limited attacks of fever with serositis. **Lifelong colchicine** prevents attacks and, most importantly, **AA amyloidosis affecting the kidneys**.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-30", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "accidents", "stem": "A 3-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department 40 minutes after her mother found her with an open bottle of her grandmother's tablets. She is asymptomatic and alert.\n\nRegarding the use of activated charcoal, which of the following statements is correct?", "options": ["It is effective if given within 6 hours of ingestion", "It is most effective within 1 hour of ingestion but ineffective for iron, hydrocarbons, and pesticides", "It should be combined with gastric lavage for maximum benefit", "It is contraindicated in asymptomatic children", "Induced vomiting should be performed first to maximise its effect"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Activated charcoal has a **high surface area that adsorbs many drugs**, and it can reduce absorption of a toxic agent **if administered within 1 hour of ingestion**. Crucially, it is **ineffective for iron, hydrocarbons, and pesticides** — substances that do not adsorb to charcoal — and giving it in these poisonings provides false reassurance.\n\nWhether to attempt reduction of absorption at all depends on four considerations: the **intrinsic toxicity** of the agent (consult the poisons information service), the **reported dose ingested**, the **presence of symptoms**, and the **time since ingestion**.\n\n*(C, E)* **Gastric lavage and induced vomiting are no longer recommended.** This is a definite change from older practice and a common exam point.\n\n*(A)* The window is 1 hour, not 6.\n\n*(D)* Being asymptomatic does not contraindicate charcoal; indeed, early presentation before symptom onset is precisely when it may be useful.\n\nRegarding investigations: general blood tests depend on the mechanism and likelihood of toxicity; ECG is obtained for drugs with cardiovascular toxicity; **specific blood concentrations are only helpful for paracetamol, iron, salicylates, and alcohol**; and **urine toxicology screening is not helpful acutely**, though it may later confirm a diagnosis.", "objective": "Activated charcoal works only if given **within 1 hour** and is **ineffective for iron, hydrocarbons, and pesticides**. Gastric lavage and induced emesis are obsolete.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-31", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "accidents", "stem": "A 2-year-old boy is found submerged in a garden pond. He is pulled out after an unknown period, is apnoeic and pulseless, and his core temperature on arrival is 29°C. His pupils are fixed and dilated. Resuscitation has been ongoing for 25 minutes.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate course of action?", "options": ["Stop resuscitation, as fixed dilated pupils indicate irreversible brain death", "Continue resuscitation until the child is rewarmed to normal body temperature", "Stop resuscitation after 30 minutes regardless of temperature", "Administer adrenaline once and then reassess for cessation", "Rewarm rapidly with hot water immersion, then reassess"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "In drowning with hypothermia, **resuscitation should continue until the child is warmed to a normal body temperature, even in the presence of fixed dilated pupils**, because recovery may still be possible. Cold water induces hypothermia, which has a **protective effect** on the brain by reducing cerebral metabolic demand — the maxim being that a drowned child is not dead until \"warm and dead.\"\n\nAdditional points on drowning: it is defined as respiratory impairment from submersion or immersion in liquid; once submerged, asphyxiation occurs **with or without aspiration of water**. **Babies and toddlers drown in domestic settings** — baths, paddling pools, garden ponds — whereas older children get into difficulty away from home in canals, lakes, and the sea. **Up to 30% of fatalities can be avoided by skilled on-scene resuscitation.**\n\n*(A, C, D)* All would terminate resuscitation prematurely in a potentially salvageable child. Fixed dilated pupils are unreliable as a prognostic sign in hypothermia.\n\n*(E)* Rewarming is necessary but must be controlled; the key principle is that resuscitation continues *during* rewarming rather than being abandoned before it.", "objective": "In hypothermic drowning, **continue resuscitation until the child is rewarmed**, regardless of fixed dilated pupils — hypothermia is neuroprotective and recovery remains possible.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-32", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "emergencies", "stem": "A 2-year-old boy with gastroenteritis is assessed in the emergency department. He is drowsy but responds to his mother's voice. His heart rate is 170/min, respiratory rate 44/min, skin is mottled with cold peripheries, capillary refill time is 4 seconds, and blood pressure is 92/56 mmHg (normal for age).\n\nWhich of the following best describes his circulatory status?", "options": ["No circulatory compromise, as blood pressure is normal", "Compensated shock", "Decompensated shock", "Cardiogenic shock", "Neurogenic shock"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "This child is in **compensated shock**. The defining insight is that **a normal blood pressure does not exclude shock in children** — indeed, **hypotension is a late feature** signifying that compensatory responses are failing. **Tachycardia is a much earlier indicator than hypotension.**\n\nIn early compensated shock, blood pressure is maintained by increased heart and respiratory rates, redistribution of blood from the venous reserve volume, and diversion of flow from non-essential tissues (skin, peripheries — hence cold, mottled extremities) toward brain and heart. All the features here fit: tachycardia, tachypnoea, mottled cold skin, and **capillary refill time >2 seconds** (prolonged).\n\nRecognising this stage matters enormously because **compensated shock is reversible, whereas decompensated shock may be irreversible**.\n\n*(C)* Decompensated shock is marked by **hypotension, bradycardia, acidotic (Kussmaul) breathing, confusion or depressed cerebral state, blue peripheries, and absent urine output**. Blood pressure here is normal.\n\n*(A)* This is the central error the question tests.\n\n*(D, E)* Shock is categorised as hypovolaemic, maldistributive (sepsis, anaphylaxis), cardiogenic (arrhythmia, heart failure), or neurogenic (spinal cord injury). This child's gastroenteritis points to hypovolaemia.", "objective": "In children, **hypotension is a late sign of shock**. Tachycardia, prolonged capillary refill, and cold mottled peripheries indicate **compensated shock**, which is reversible if treated promptly.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-33", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "emergencies", "stem": "An 8-year-old girl weighing 25 kg presents in septic shock. She has received three 10 mL/kg boluses of balanced crystalloid with only transient improvement, and remains tachycardic with prolonged capillary refill.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate next consideration?", "options": ["Continue crystalloid boluses indefinitely until perfusion normalises", "Call for a clinician with advanced airway skills, as mechanical ventilation is likely required", "Switch to hypotonic maintenance fluid", "Withhold further fluid and observe", "Give a 20 mL/kg bolus of packed red cells"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "**Children who require more than 40 mL/kg of resuscitation fluid are likely to require mechanical ventilation**, and **a healthcare professional with advanced airway skills should be called for help if multiple fluid boluses are needed**. This child has received 30 mL/kg with inadequate response and is heading toward that threshold.\n\nThe physiology behind this: in sepsis, severe hypovolaemia results from **maldistribution of intravascular protein and fluid due to \"capillary leak\"** — endothelial dysfunction driven by vasoactive inflammatory mediators. **Capillary leak into the lungs causes pulmonary oedema**, producing respiratory failure. Simultaneously, **inflammatory cytokines and circulating toxins depress myocardial contractility**, often necessitating inotropic support.\n\nResuscitation principles: boluses of **10 mL/kg** of balanced isotonic crystalloid (Plasmalyte, Hartmann's) or 0.9% NaCl, with **reassessment of circulatory adequacy after each bolus** to avoid fluid overload. In **trauma**, replacement is with **blood and blood products at 5–10 mL/kg**. Note also that **febrile children should not receive fluid boluses unless they show clinical signs of shock**.\n\n*(A)* Indefinite fluid without reassessment risks fluid overload and pulmonary oedema.\n\n*(C)* Hypotonic fluid is inappropriate for resuscitation.\n\n*(D)* Withholding treatment in ongoing shock is unsafe.\n\n*(E)* Blood is indicated in trauma or haemorrhage, not in septic shock at this stage.", "objective": "Reassess after every 10 mL/kg bolus. Beyond **40 mL/kg**, anticipate the need for **mechanical ventilation and intensive care** — summon advanced airway support early.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-34", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "emergencies", "stem": "A 4-year-old boy has been having a generalised tonic-clonic seizure for 6 minutes. Intravenous access has not been established. He has received no anticonvulsant so far.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate immediate treatment?", "options": ["Intravenous lorazepam", "Buccal midazolam or rectal diazepam", "Intravenous phenytoin", "Rectal paraldehyde", "Rapid sequence induction with thiopental"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "With **no intravenous access**, the first-line benzodiazepine is **buccal midazolam or rectal diazepam**. Where IV or IO access is available or can be established quickly, **lorazepam** is used instead. This is step 1 of the status epilepticus algorithm, given at **5 minutes** of ongoing seizure.\n\nThe subsequent steps escalate on a timed basis: a second dose of benzodiazepine at step 2 (with senior help called and phenytoin prepared), **phenytoin IV/IO over 20 minutes** at step 3 — or **phenobarbitone** if the child is already on phenytoin — with **paraldehyde PR** considered while phenytoin is being prepared or infused, and finally at **45 minutes**, rapid sequence induction of anaesthesia with **thiopental**, which requires an anaesthetist to be present.\n\nTwo principles govern management. First, **\"don't ever forget glucose\"** — check blood glucose and treat reversible causes such as hypoglycaemia or electrolyte disturbance. Second, the aim is to prevent a **prolonged seizure (≥5 minutes)** from progressing to status epilepticus, defined as a **continuous seizure >30 minutes**, or intermittent seizures over 30 minutes without full recovery of consciousness between them. Longer seizures carry a **poorer outcome and become more resistant to treatment**.\n\n*(A)* Correct agent if access existed, but it does not.\n\n*(C, D, E)* These are later steps in the algorithm.", "objective": "Status epilepticus step 1 at 5 minutes: **lorazepam IV/IO if access available; otherwise buccal midazolam or rectal diazepam**. Always check glucose. Thiopental with an anaesthetist present at 45 minutes.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-35", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "emergencies", "stem": "A 6-year-old boy develops acute urticaria, swelling of the lips and tongue, stridor, and wheeze 10 minutes after eating a biscuit at a birthday party. He is pale with cold extremities and feels faint.\n\nWhich of the following is the single most important immediate intervention?", "options": ["Intravenous hydrocortisone", "Intramuscular adrenaline", "Oral chlorphenamine", "Nebulised salbutamol", "Intravenous fluid challenge"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "**The acute management of anaphylaxis relies on early administration of intramuscular adrenaline**, repeated **every 5 minutes until clinical improvement**. Nothing else substitutes for it, and delay is the principal determinant of fatality.\n\nThe diagnosis rests on **acute onset of illness** with **life-threatening problems** in one or more systems: **airway** (swelling, hoarseness, stridor), **breathing** (tachypnoea, wheeze, fatigue, cyanosis, SpO₂ <92%), **circulation** (pale, cold extremities, weak pulses, low blood pressure), or **disability** (feeling faint, drowsy, coma). Skin changes — generalised urticaria, swollen lips, tongue, and uvula — are **usually but not always** present.\n\nImmediate adjuncts: call for help, **lie the patient flat and raise the legs**. Once specialist equipment is available: establish airway, high-flow oxygen, IV fluid challenge, chlorphenamine, and hydrocortisone. An **intravenous adrenaline infusion** is considered only if **three cycles of intramuscular adrenaline fail**, given under anaesthetic supervision.\n\nEpidemiology worth knowing: in children **approximately two-thirds of anaphylaxis is caused by food allergy**; insect stings and medications are far less common than in adults. **Most paediatric anaphylaxis occurs under 5 years, but the majority of fatalities occur in adolescents**, particularly with **milk** allergy, followed by peanut and tree nuts. **Asthma is an additional risk factor**, and no trigger is identified in about 20%. Approximately **1 in 1000 episodes is fatal**. Elevated serum **mast cell tryptase** may support the diagnosis, but a negative test does not exclude it.\n\n*(A, C, D, E)* All are secondary measures given after adrenaline.", "objective": "Anaphylaxis is treated with **immediate intramuscular adrenaline**, repeated every 5 minutes. Antihistamines and steroids are adjuncts, never substitutes.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-36", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "emergencies", "stem": "A previously well 5-week-old infant born at 39 weeks' gestation had a 30-second episode at home during which he became pale and floppy with irregular breathing, resolving spontaneously without intervention. He is now alert, feeding normally, with a completely normal examination and normal vital signs. This is the first such episode.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": ["Discharge immediately with reassurance only", "Admission for detailed investigation including lumbar puncture and CT head", "Full septic screen and empirical antibiotics", "This does not meet low-risk criteria; more detailed observation and investigation are required", "Home apnoea monitor and outpatient follow-up"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "This infant fails the low-risk criteria for a **brief resolved unexplained event (BRUE)** on a single point: **age must be >60 days**, and he is only 5 weeks (35 days) old. Infants who do **not** meet all low-risk criteria require **more detailed observation and investigation**, as the episode may reflect a serious underlying cause.\n\nThe full **low-risk BRUE criteria** are:\n- **Age >60 days**\n- **Born at ≥32 weeks' gestation**\n- **First BRUE**\n- **Event lasting <1 minute**\n- **No CPR given by a trained medical provider**\n- **No concerning features in history or examination**\n\nA BRUE is defined as a sudden, transient episode lasting **less than one minute** consisting of one or more of: cyanosis or pallor; absent, decreased, or irregular breathing; change in tone; or altered responsiveness. The terminology replaced \"apparent life-threatening event\" (ALTE) specifically to reflect that the label rests on the **clinician's assessment** of the event rather than the caregiver's perception that it was life-threatening.\n\nFor genuinely **low-risk** BRUEs, management is limited to a period of observation and monitoring of vital signs, with detailed explanation to caregivers and basic life support training offered. An ECG, pernasal swab for pertussis, and continuous pulse oximetry may be considered — but **other laboratory and imaging studies are unlikely to help and should not be performed**.\n\n*(A, E)* Inappropriate given he is outside low-risk criteria.\n\n*(B, C)* Investigation should be guided by clinical findings rather than reflexively comprehensive.", "objective": "Low-risk BRUE requires **age >60 days, ≥32 weeks' gestation, first event, <1 minute, no CPR by a trained provider, and no concerning features**. Failing any criterion mandates fuller assessment.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-37", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "emergencies", "stem": "Parents of a newborn ask what they can do to reduce the risk of sudden infant death syndrome.\n\nWhich of the following is the single most effective intervention?", "options": ["Use of a home apnoea monitor", "Placing the baby supine (on the back) to sleep", "Swaddling the infant tightly with a duvet", "Sharing a bed to allow closer monitoring", "Keeping the room temperature above 22°C"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The **\"Back to Sleep\"** campaign — advising that infants be placed **on their backs to sleep** rather than prone — produced a dramatic fall in SIDS incidence in the UK, from **1.9 per 1000 live births in 1989 to 0.3 per 1000 in 2018**. No other single measure has had comparable impact.\n\nAdditional evidence-based advice includes: **feet at the foot of the cot** with head uncovered and blanket tucked under the arms, or use of a baby sleeping bag; a **firm, flat mattress** with a **clear sleep space** — no pillows or duvets; the baby in a **separate cot in the parents' room for the first 6 months**; **avoiding overheating**, aiming for a room temperature of **18°C**; **avoiding smoking during pregnancy** and maintaining a smoke-free environment; **avoiding co-sleeping**, particularly after alcohol or sedatives or if a smoker; **breastfeeding**; and seeking medical advice promptly if the infant is unwell.\n\nEpidemiology: peak age is **2–4 months**, though it occurs throughout the first year; **55% are boys**; risk is increased fivefold in **low birthweight** infants and fivefold with **maternal age under 20**. SIDS is the most common cause of death between 1 month and 1 year. In most cases no cause is found even after detailed autopsy, though occasionally an undiagnosed congenital abnormality or inborn error of metabolism is identified.\n\n*(C, D, E)* Each is a recognised **risk factor** — duvets and pillows, co-sleeping, and overheating all increase risk.\n\n*(A)* Home apnoea monitors have not been shown to reduce SIDS.", "objective": "Supine sleeping position is the single most effective SIDS prevention measure. Peak incidence is **2–4 months**; other measures include a clear cot, room temperature 18°C, avoiding smoking and co-sleeping, and breastfeeding.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-38", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "emergencies", "stem": "A 10-month-old infant is choking on a piece of food. He is conscious but has an ineffective cough and cannot cry.\n\nWhich of the following is the correct immediate manoeuvre?", "options": ["5 abdominal thrusts", "5 back blows followed by 5 chest thrusts", "Blind finger sweep of the oropharynx", "Immediate chest compressions", "Encourage coughing and observe"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "In an **infant** (under 1 year) with **severe airway obstruction** — an ineffective cough, as here — the sequence is **5 back blows followed by 5 chest thrusts**. **Abdominal thrusts are avoided in infants because of the risk of injury to the liver and spleen**, both of which are relatively large and poorly protected by the rib cage at this age.\n\nIn **children over 1 year**, **5 abdominal thrusts (Heimlich manoeuvre)** are used: a fist is placed against the upper abdomen in the midline, grasped with the other hand, and pulled backwards and upwards to expel air from the lungs.\n\nThe algorithm branches first on severity: with **mild obstruction (effective cough)**, encourage coughing and continue to check for deterioration until the obstruction clears or the cough becomes effective. With **severe obstruction (ineffective cough)**, the next branch is conscious versus unconscious — if **unconscious, start CPR**.\n\n*(C)* **Blind finger sweep is not advised** — foreign matter should be removed only under direct vision, as blind sweeping may impact the object further.\n\n*(E)* Appropriate only for mild obstruction with an effective cough.\n\nBackground: young children are prone to choking because they put objects in their mouths and their airway diameter is small. **Food is the most common cause of non-fatal choking, followed by toys.**", "objective": "Infant choking (severe): **5 back blows + 5 chest thrusts** — never abdominal thrusts (liver/spleen injury). Child >1 year: **5 abdominal thrusts**. Never perform a blind finger sweep.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-39", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "emergencies", "stem": "During basic life support of a 3-year-old child who is unresponsive with no signs of life, a single trained rescuer with no equipment is present.\n\nWhich of the following correctly describes the technique?", "options": ["30 compressions to 2 breaths, rate 100–120/min, check carotid pulse", "5 rescue breaths first, then 15 compressions to 2 breaths, rate 100–120/min, check carotid or femoral pulse", "2 rescue breaths first, then 30:2, rate 80/min, check brachial pulse", "Immediate defibrillation before any compressions", "15 compressions to 2 breaths without initial rescue breaths"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Paediatric basic life support differs from adult BLS in ways that reflect the different aetiology of arrest. **In adults, cardiopulmonary arrest is usually cardiac in origin, secondary to ischaemic heart disease. In children, it is usually secondary to hypoxia** from respiratory or neurological failure or shock. This is why **5 rescue breaths are given first**, before compressions.\n\nThe sequence: approach with care, check responsiveness (**do not shake infants or those with suspected cervical spine injury**), open the airway with **head tilt–chin lift** (or **jaw thrust** if unsuccessful), check breathing for a maximum of 10 seconds, give **5 rescue breaths**, then check for signs of life and pulse for a maximum of 10 seconds — **carotid or femoral in children over 1 year; brachial or femoral in infants under 1 year**. If there are no signs of life (unless there is a definite pulse >60/min), begin chest compressions at a ratio of **15 compressions to 2 breaths**, rate **100–120/min**, pushing \"hard and fast.\"\n\nIf alone, **give 1 minute of resuscitation before seeking help**, ideally using a mobile phone on speaker.\n\n*(A, C)* 30:2 is the adult single-rescuer ratio; the paediatric ratio is 15:2.\n\n*(D)* Defibrillation is prioritised in witnessed sudden collapse in adults, reflecting the cardiac aetiology — not the default in children.\n\n*(E)* Omits the 5 initial rescue breaths, which are essential given the hypoxic aetiology.", "objective": "Paediatric BLS: **5 rescue breaths first**, then **15:2** at **100–120/min**. Pulse check: carotid/femoral if >1 year, **brachial/femoral if <1 year**. Arrest in children is usually **hypoxic**, not cardiac.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-40", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "perinatal", "stem": "A woman with poorly controlled type 1 diabetes delivers a baby vaginally at 36 weeks' gestation weighing 5.5 kg. Delivery was complicated by shoulder dystocia, and the infant has a right-sided brachial plexus injury. He appears plethoric.\n\nWhich of the following best explains the macrosomia?", "options": ["Maternal insulin crosses the placenta and stimulates fetal growth", "Maternal hyperglycaemia causes fetal hyperglycaemia, and fetal insulin promotes growth", "Fetal growth hormone secretion is increased by maternal hyperglycaemia", "Placental lactogen is directly anabolic to fetal tissues", "Maternal hyperglycaemia causes fetal hypoxia with compensatory growth"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "**Glucose crosses the placenta; insulin does not.** Maternal hyperglycaemia therefore produces fetal hyperglycaemia, and the fetus responds with **increased secretion of its own insulin**. Insulin is a potent fetal growth factor, promoting growth by increasing **both cell number and cell size**. About **25% of infants of diabetic mothers are macrosomic** (birthweight >4 kg) compared with 8% of non-diabetics.\n\nThe consequences follow: macrosomia predisposes to **cephalopelvic disproportion, birth asphyxia, shoulder dystocia, and brachial plexus injury** — the last two both present here.\n\nThe same hyperinsulinism explains the **neonatal** problems: **transient hypoglycaemia is common during the first day of life** and is prevented by early feeding, with blood glucose monitored closely over the first 24 hours. Other neonatal issues include **respiratory distress syndrome** (surfactant maturation is delayed), **hypertrophic cardiomyopathy** (septal hypertrophy, which regresses over weeks but may cause heart failure), and **polycythaemia** (venous haematocrit >0.65), producing the plethoric appearance described.\n\nFetal problems also include a **6% risk of congenital malformation — a fourfold increase** — particularly **cardiac malformations**, and more rarely **sacral agenesis (caudal regression)** and hypoplastic left colon. Good **periconceptional** control reduces this risk. Conversely, mothers with long-standing **microvascular disease** show a **threefold increase in growth restriction**.\n\n*(A)* Insulin does not cross the placenta — this is the crux.\n\n*(C, D, E)* None describes the established mechanism.", "objective": "Glucose crosses the placenta, insulin does not. Fetal hyperinsulinism causes **macrosomia**, then **neonatal hypoglycaemia**, polycythaemia, RDS, and hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-41", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "perinatal", "stem": "A pregnant woman is found to have seroconverted to rubella. She wishes to know the likely consequences for her fetus.\n\nAt which gestational age does maternal rubella infection carry the highest risk of severe fetal damage?", "options": ["Before 8 weeks", "13–16 weeks", "18–22 weeks", "24–28 weeks", "After 30 weeks"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The risk and extent of fetal damage in congenital rubella are determined principally by **gestational age at the onset of maternal infection**, and risk is **highest earliest**:\n\n- **Before 8 weeks:** deafness, congenital heart disease, and cataracts occur in **over 80%** of cases\n- **13–16 weeks:** approximately **30%** of fetuses have impaired hearing\n- **Beyond 18 weeks:** the risk to the fetus is **minimal**\n\nImportantly, **viraemia after birth continues to damage the infant**, so the process does not end at delivery.\n\nDiagnosis of maternal infection **must be confirmed serologically**, as clinical diagnosis is unreliable. Confirmatory testing includes maternal seroconversion on screening serology, PCR on amniocentesis or chorionic villus sample, PCR of infant urine, and **rubella-specific IgM** in infant blood.\n\n**Congenital rubella is preventable** and has become extremely rare in the UK since MMR vaccination was introduced — but this depends entirely on maintaining a high vaccine uptake rate, which makes it a live public health issue rather than a historical one.\n\n*(B)* Carries substantial but lower risk, confined largely to hearing.\n\n*(C, D, E)* Risk beyond 18 weeks is minimal.", "objective": "Congenital rubella risk is inversely related to gestational age: **>80% severely affected if infection occurs before 8 weeks** (deafness, congenital heart disease, cataracts); **minimal risk after 18 weeks**.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-42", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "perinatal", "stem": "A newborn infant is noted to have hepatosplenomegaly and a petechial rash. Investigations confirm the most common congenital infection in the UK.\n\nWhich of the following statements about this infection is correct?", "options": ["It affects approximately 5% of live births", "90% of infected infants are normal at birth and develop normally", "Pregnant women are routinely screened for it", "An effective vaccine is available", "Treatment is with pyrimethamine and sulfadiazine"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Congenital **cytomegalovirus** is the most common congenital infection, affecting **0.5–1 per 1000 live births** in the UK. The outcome distribution is a classic exam fact:\n\n- **90% are normal at birth and develop normally**\n- **5% have clinical features at birth** — hepatosplenomegaly, petechiae — and **most of these** develop neurodevelopmental disability: sensorineural hearing loss, cerebral palsy, epilepsy, cognitive impairment\n- **5% develop problems later in life**, mainly **sensorineural hearing loss**\n\nCMV is **the most common non-genetic cause of sensorineural hearing loss.**\n\nIn Europe **50% of pregnant women are susceptible**; about **1% of susceptible women** have a primary infection in pregnancy, and in about **40% of these the infant becomes infected**. The mother is usually asymptomatic or has a mild non-specific illness. Infection may also follow recurrent maternal infection, though transmission rates are lower.\n\n*(C, D)* **There is no CMV vaccine, and pregnant women are not screened for CMV** — an important contrast with rubella.\n\n*(E)* Pyrimethamine and sulfadiazine treat congenital **toxoplasmosis** (for 1 year). CMV is treated with **oral valganciclovir**, and early antiviral treatment of infants with sensorineural hearing loss or CNS involvement reduces the adverse impact on hearing and long-term neurodevelopment.\n\n*(A)* The incidence is per 1000, not 5%.", "objective": "Congenital CMV — commonest congenital infection; **90% normal, 5% symptomatic at birth, 5% late problems (mainly hearing loss)**. No vaccine, no screening. Treat with **valganciclovir**.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-43", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "perinatal", "stem": "A term infant is born apnoeic and floppy following a prolonged second stage. At 1 minute the heart rate is 80/min, there is no respiratory effort, muscle tone is flaccid, there is a grimace to stimulation, and the body is blue.\n\nWhat is the Apgar score?", "options": ["1", "2", "3", "4", "5"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Scoring each component:\n- **Heart rate** 80/min (<100) = **1**\n- **Respiratory effort** absent = **0**\n- **Muscle tone** flaccid = **0**\n- **Reflex irritability** grimace = **1**\n- **Colour** blue = **0**\n\n**Total = 2.**\n\nThe Apgar score describes a baby's condition at **1 minute and 5 minutes** after delivery, and at 5-minute intervals thereafter if the condition remains poor. **The most important components are heart rate and respiration.**\n\nFull scoring:\n\n| Component | 0 | 1 | 2 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Heart rate | Absent | <100/min | ≥100/min |\n| Respiratory effort | Absent | Gasping/irregular | Regular, strong cry |\n| Muscle tone | Flaccid | Some flexion | Well flexed, active |\n| Reflex irritability | None | Grimace | Cry, cough |\n| Colour | Pale/blue | Body pink, extremities blue | Pink |\n\nUnderstanding the underlying physiology matters more than the arithmetic. With continuing asphyxia the fetus first gasps, then enters **primary apnoea** — during which **the heart rate is maintained**. If deprivation continues, irregular gasping resumes, followed by **secondary apnoea**, when **heart rate and blood pressure fall**. An infant delivered in secondary apnoea **will only recover if resuscitation with lung expansion is provided**.", "objective": "Apgar assesses heart rate, respiration, tone, reflex irritability, and colour at 1 and 5 minutes; **heart rate and respiration are most important**. Secondary apnoea (falling heart rate) requires lung inflation to recover.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-44", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "perinatal", "stem": "A term infant is not breathing at birth. He has been dried and the airway opened, and five inflation breaths have been given. After 30 seconds of effective ventilation with visible chest movement, the heart rate remains 45/min.\n\nWhich of the following is the correct next step?", "options": ["Continue ventilation alone and reassess in 60 seconds", "Chest compressions at a ratio of 15:2", "Chest compressions at a ratio of 3:1, increase inspired oxygen to 100%", "Immediate intravenous adrenaline", "Stop resuscitation"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "**Chest compressions are started if the heart rate is below 60/min despite effective lung inflation.** The neonatal ratio is **3 compressions to 1 inflation**, at a rate of **90 compressions to 30 breaths per minute (120 events per minute)**, avoiding compression during a ventilation breath. **Inspired oxygen is increased to 100%.** The heart rate is rechecked every 30 seconds, and compressions stop when it exceeds 60/min.\n\nNote how this differs from paediatric BLS beyond the newborn period (15:2) — the 3:1 ratio reflects the overwhelmingly **respiratory** aetiology of neonatal compromise.\n\nTechnique: pressure is applied to the **lower third of the sternum**, just below an imaginary line joining the nipples, depressing the antero-posterior diameter by **one-third (1–1.5 cm)**. The **thumb technique with hands encircling the chest** is preferred; the two-finger technique is less effective but easier if alone.\n\nThe overarching principle: **providing effective lung aeration — shown by improvement in heart rate and good chest wall movement — is the key to successful neonatal resuscitation.** Drugs are needed in fewer than 1 in 1000 births, and evidence for their efficacy is poor.\n\n*(B)* 15:2 is the paediatric ratio outside the newborn period.\n\n*(D)* Adrenaline is considered only if the heart rate remains <60/min **despite adequate ventilation and chest compression**, ideally via umbilical venous catheter (IV 0.2 mL/kg of 1:10,000).\n\n*(E)* Resuscitation is stopped only after **10 minutes of effective resuscitation with no breathing or cardiac output**, and that decision rests with a senior paediatrician.", "objective": "Neonatal resuscitation: if heart rate <60/min after effective ventilation, begin compressions at **3:1**, 120 events/min, with 100% oxygen. Effective **lung aeration** is the key to success.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-45", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "perinatal", "stem": "An infant is delivered at 28 weeks' gestation. The resuscitation team is preparing for delivery.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate approach to thermal care and initial oxygen concentration?", "options": ["Dry thoroughly, wrap in warm towels, resuscitate in 100% oxygen", "Place directly into a plastic bag without drying under a radiant warmer, start resuscitation in 30% oxygen", "Dry thoroughly, place in plastic bag, start in air (21%)", "Delay all thermal measures until the airway is secured, use 100% oxygen", "Immerse in a warm bath, use 50% oxygen"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "**Thermal care:** infants **less than 32 weeks' gestation** should be placed **directly into a plastic bag or wrapped in clear plastic sheeting without drying**, so the plastic clings to the skin and acts as an additional layer preventing **evaporative heat loss**. The **head is left exposed and covered with a hat**, and a radiant warmer, thermal mattress, and warmed humidified gases may be used. **Delivery room temperature should be 26°C for very preterm infants.**\n\nThe stakes are high: hypothermia increases morbidity and mortality, and **every 1°C below the target range on neonatal unit admission in very preterm infants increases mortality by 28%.** Target temperature is **36.5–37.5°C** unless therapeutic hypothermia is planned.\n\n**Oxygen:** excessive tissue oxygenation causes damage to brain, lungs, and eyes via **oxygen free radicals**. Initial concentrations are therefore graded by gestation: **air (21%) if >32 weeks; 21–30% if 28–31 weeks; 30% if <28 weeks**. An air/oxygen blender is used and additional oxygen titrated against saturation, **not exceeding a preductal saturation of 95%**.\n\nNote that at 28 weeks, either 21–30% (by the 28–31 week band) is defensible; the essential principle being tested is **low-concentration oxygen, not 100%**.\n\nPreterm infants under 32 weeks may also benefit from **CPAP** to maintain lung aeration and avoid intubation, and very premature infants may need **early surfactant**.\n\n*(A, D, E)* All use inappropriately high oxygen; (A) and (C) involve drying, which defeats the purpose of the plastic wrap.", "objective": "For infants <32 weeks: **plastic bag without drying**, head covered, radiant warmer, room at 26°C. Start resuscitation in **low-concentration oxygen** (21–30%), never 100%.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-46", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "perinatal", "stem": "A term infant is delivered through thick meconium-stained liquor. At birth he is floppy and not breathing.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": ["Suction the nose and mouth while the head is on the perineum", "Immediate intubation and tracheal suction before any ventilation", "Brief inspection of the oropharynx with removal of thick meconium, but positive pressure ventilation is the priority", "Withhold ventilation until meconium is fully cleared to avoid pushing it into the lungs", "Chest compressions before any airway intervention"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The governing principle is that **positive pressure ventilation to aerate the lungs is the priority despite the presence of meconium**. In a floppy, non-breathing infant born through thick meconium, it is **reasonable to briefly inspect the oropharynx and remove thick meconium by suctioning with a large-bore catheter**, but this must not delay lung inflation. **Initiating lung inflation within the first minute of life is the priority.**\n\n*(A)* **Attempting to aspirate meconium from the nose and mouth while the head is on the perineum is not recommended, as it is ineffective** — a practice that has been abandoned.\n\n*(B, D)* Both delay ventilation, which is the intervention that determines outcome.\n\n*(E)* Compressions precede nothing; ventilation comes first in the neonatal sequence.\n\nAdditional context: passage of meconium becomes **increasingly common with greater gestational age, particularly post-term**. Infants who become hypoxaemic may inhale thick meconium and develop **meconium aspiration syndrome**. Critically, **if the infant cries at birth and establishes regular respiration, no resuscitation is required** regardless of meconium staining.\n\nGeneral airway point: **routine suction of the mouth and nose is usually unnecessary**, and **vigorous suction of the back of the throat may provoke bradycardia through vagal stimulation** and should be avoided.", "objective": "In meconium-stained liquor with a non-breathing infant, **lung inflation takes priority**. Brief oropharyngeal suction under direct vision is acceptable; perineal suctioning is obsolete; vigorous pharyngeal suction risks vagal bradycardia.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-47", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "perinatal", "stem": "A baby is born at term with a birthweight on the 3rd centile. Antenatal scans showed a head circumference on the 50th centile with abdominal circumference on the 2nd centile. The mother has pre-eclampsia and smokes.\n\nWhich of the following statements about this growth pattern is correct?", "options": ["This is symmetrical growth restriction, suggesting a genetic disorder or congenital infection", "This is asymmetrical growth restriction from late uteroplacental dysfunction; catch-up growth is expected", "This pattern indicates the infant is constitutionally small and requires no follow-up", "Symmetrical restriction of this type is associated with maternal pre-eclampsia", "This infant is at low risk of neonatal hypoglycaemia"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "**Asymmetrical** growth restriction — the more common pattern — is defined by the **weight or abdominal circumference lying on a lower centile than the head**, exactly as described. **Brain growth is relatively spared at the expense of liver glycogen and skin fat** (\"head-sparing\").\n\nThe cause is **failure of the placenta to provide adequate nutrition late in pregnancy (uteroplacental dysfunction)**, secondary to **maternal pre-eclampsia, maternal smoking**, or idiopathic — both risk factors present here. **These infants exhibit catch-up growth during childhood**, though this, together with early-life programming in utero, predisposes to **metabolic syndrome** in later life (the DOHaD hypothesis).\n\n*(A, D)* **Symmetrical** growth restriction — head and abdominal circumference **equally reduced** — suggests a prolonged period of poor growth beginning **early in pregnancy**. It is usually a small but normal fetus, but may reflect a **fetal genetic disorder or syndrome, congenital infection, maternal drug or alcohol misuse, maternal chronic disease, or malnutrition**. These infants **are more likely to remain permanently small**.\n\n*(C)* Growth-restricted infants require monitoring.\n\n*(E)* Growth-restricted infants are specifically liable to **hypothermia** (relatively large surface area, especially the head), **hypoglycaemia** (poor fat and glycogen stores), **hypocalcaemia**, and **polycythaemia** (haematocrit >0.65).\n\n**Small for gestational age** is defined as birthweight **<10th centile**; large for gestational age **>90th centile**.", "objective": "**Asymmetrical** IUGR (head spared, abdomen small) = late uteroplacental failure (pre-eclampsia, smoking); catch-up growth expected. **Symmetrical** IUGR = early insult (genetic, congenital infection, alcohol); often permanently small.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-48", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "perinatal", "stem": "During the newborn infant physical examination of a 12-hour-old term baby, the red reflex is absent in the left eye.\n\nWhich of the following is the most important diagnosis to exclude?", "options": ["Congenital glaucoma", "Retinoblastoma", "Conjunctivitis", "Nasolacrimal duct obstruction", "Ptosis"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "An **absent red reflex** may be caused by **cataracts, retinoblastoma, or corneal opacity**. Of these, **retinoblastoma** is the diagnosis that must not be missed — it is a malignant tumour where delayed detection threatens both vision and life. The reflex can be harder to elicit in darker-skinned infants, but the **retinal vessels can still be visualised**.\n\nThe **newborn infant physical examination (NIPE)** must be performed **within 72 hours of birth** on every baby, and is repeated at **6–8 weeks** by the general practitioner. Its purpose is to detect congenital abnormalities not already identified — eye abnormalities, congenital heart disease, undescended testes, developmental dysplasia of the hip — to check for problems arising from maternal or familial disease, and to give parents an opportunity to ask questions.\n\nOther high-yield NIPE points:\n- **Jaundice within 24 hours of birth requires further evaluation** — it is never physiological\n- **Femoral pulses:** pulse pressure is **reduced in coarctation of the aorta**, **increased with a patent ductus arteriosus**\n- **Palate** must be both visually inspected posteriorly (posterior cleft) and **palpated** (submucous cleft)\n- Normal heart rate is **110–160/min**, dropping to 85/min in sleep\n- A **tense fontanelle when not crying** suggests raised intracranial pressure — perform cranial ultrasound\n- **Hips are examined last** because the procedure is uncomfortable\n- **Central cyanosis**, best seen on the tongue, always needs urgent assessment", "objective": "An absent red reflex at NIPE requires urgent ophthalmological referral to exclude **retinoblastoma**, cataract, and corneal opacity. NIPE is performed within **72 hours** and repeated at 6–8 weeks.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-49", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "neonatal", "stem": "A male infant is born at 27 weeks' gestation. Within 2 hours he develops tachypnoea of 75 breaths/min, sternal and subcostal recession, nasal flaring, and expiratory grunting. Chest X-ray shows a diffuse granular \"ground glass\" appearance with an air bronchogram and an indistinct heart border.\n\nWhich of the following best explains the expiratory grunting?", "options": ["Partial laryngeal obstruction from airway oedema", "An attempt to generate positive airway pressure during expiration to maintain functional residual capacity", "Bronchospasm from inhaled amniotic debris", "Compensatory hyperventilation for metabolic acidosis", "Vocal cord paralysis from recurrent laryngeal nerve injury"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "**Expiratory grunting is an attempt to create positive airway pressure during expiration and maintain functional residual capacity.** The infant partially closes the glottis during expiration, generating auto-PEEP that splints open alveoli which would otherwise collapse. Understanding this explains why **CPAP is the logical therapy** — it does mechanically what the infant is attempting physiologically.\n\nThis is respiratory distress syndrome (hyaline membrane disease). **Surfactant is a mixture of phospholipids and proteins secreted by type II pneumocytes** of the alveolar epithelium; it lowers surface tension. Deficiency causes **widespread alveolar collapse and inadequate gas exchange**. Histology shows a proteinaceous exudate forming a **hyaline membrane** in the airways. Incidence rises with decreasing gestation, is very common before 28 weeks, and is **more severe in boys**.\n\nClinical signs appear **at delivery or within 4 hours**: tachypnoea >60/min, chest wall recession, nasal flaring, expiratory grunting, and cyanosis if severe. The chest X-ray described — **diffuse granular/ground-glass appearance with air bronchogram** — is characteristic.\n\nManagement: supplemental oxygen and **non-invasive support (CPAP or high-flow nasal cannula)** first, **surfactant** instilled via tracheal tube or fine catheter between the vocal cords, and mechanical ventilation if response is inadequate. **Non-invasive support is preferred whenever possible as it has fewer complications.** Prevention is with **antenatal glucocorticoids**, which significantly reduce RDS, bronchopulmonary dysplasia, intraventricular haemorrhage, and mortality.", "objective": "Expiratory grunting generates positive end-expiratory pressure to maintain functional residual capacity. RDS = surfactant deficiency from type II pneumocytes; X-ray shows ground-glass opacification with air bronchograms.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-50", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "neonatal", "stem": "A preterm infant at 26 weeks' gestation is receiving oxygen therapy. The team is setting saturation targets.\n\nWhich of the following correctly states the risks of saturation outside the target range in preterm infants?", "options": ["Saturations <91% increase retinopathy of prematurity; >95% increase necrotising enterocolitis", "Saturations <91% increase necrotising enterocolitis and death; >95% increase retinopathy of prematurity", "Saturations should be maintained above 95% at all times to prevent brain injury", "Oxygen saturation targets are identical for preterm and term infants", "Saturations below 91% have no adverse consequences if the infant is asymptomatic"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The target range in preterm infants is **91–95%**, and the risks at either extreme are asymmetric but both serious:\n\n- **Low saturations (<91%)** — increased risk of **necrotising enterocolitis and death**\n- **High saturations (>95%)** — increased risk of **retinopathy of prematurity**\n\nThe mechanism for hyperoxic injury is **damage to lungs, brain, and eyes from oxygen free radicals**. This is why neonatal resuscitation now begins with **21–30% oxygen in preterm infants** rather than 100%.\n\n**Retinopathy of prematurity** affects developing vessels at the junction of vascularised and non-vascularised retina, with vascular proliferation that may progress to **retinal detachment, fibrosis, and blindness**. Screening by an ophthalmologist is performed for infants **<1500 g birthweight or <32 weeks' gestation**. Even with careful oxygen monitoring, ROP is still found in about **35% of very-low-birthweight infants**, with **5% requiring treatment**; severe bilateral visual impairment occurs in about **1%**, mostly in infants under 28 weeks. Treatment is **laser therapy**; intravitreal anti-VEGF is under investigation.\n\n*(D)* Targets differ: **>95% in term infants**, 91–95% in preterm.\n\n*(A)* Reverses the associations.", "objective": "Preterm oxygen saturation target is **91–95%**. Below 91% → NEC and death; above 95% → retinopathy of prematurity. Screen for ROP if **<1500 g or <32 weeks**.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-51", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "neonatal", "stem": "A 10-day-old infant born at 27 weeks' gestation, currently on formula feeds, develops feed intolerance with bile-stained vomiting. His abdomen is distended with shiny, discoloured overlying skin, and there is fresh blood on the nappy. He rapidly becomes shocked.\n\nWhich radiological finding would confirm the diagnosis?", "options": ["Double bubble sign", "Intramural gas in the bowel wall and gas in the portal venous tract", "Ground-glass appearance of the lungs", "Dilated loops with absence of rectal gas only", "Coffee-bean sign"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Necrotising enterocolitis is confirmed by **distended bowel loops with thickening of the bowel wall containing intramural gas (pneumatosis intestinalis), and gas in the portal venous tract**. Free air under the diaphragm indicates progression to **bowel perforation**. The unifying concept is **gas-producing bacteria within the bowel wall and portal venous system**.\n\nIncidence is **inversely proportional to gestational age**, typically in the first few weeks of life, with a **mortality of about 20%**. Aetiology involves **ischaemic injury, bacterial invasion of the bowel wall, and an altered gut microbiome** — which is **improved by breast milk** (and possibly pre/probiotics) and **adversely affected by formula feeds, unduly rapid increases in enteral feeds, and antibiotics**. Risk factors include **intrauterine growth restriction, especially with antenatal reversed end-diastolic flow on Doppler**, and perinatal asphyxia.\n\nManagement: **stop oral feeding, broad-spectrum antibiotics covering aerobes and anaerobes, parenteral nutrition**, with mechanical ventilation and circulatory support often required. **Surgery** is performed for perforation, difficulty with ventilation, or failure of medical management. Long-term sequelae include **bowel strictures, malabsorption after extensive resection, and poorer neurodevelopmental outcome**.\n\n*(A)* Double bubble indicates duodenal atresia. *(C)* Describes RDS. *(D)* Suggests distal obstruction such as Hirschsprung disease. *(E)* Coffee-bean sign is associated with sigmoid volvulus.", "objective": "NEC — X-ray shows **intramural gas (pneumatosis) and portal venous gas**. Breast milk is protective; formula, rapid feed advancement, and IUGR with reversed end-diastolic flow increase risk. Mortality ~20%.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-52", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "neonatal", "stem": "A 3-day-old infant born at 25 weeks' gestation has a cranial ultrasound showing a large haemorrhage arising above the caudate nucleus and extending into the lateral ventricle, with unilateral parenchymal extension on the right.\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely long-term consequence of the parenchymal component?", "options": ["Spastic diplegia", "Hemiplegia", "Choreoathetoid cerebral palsy", "Sensorineural hearing loss", "Complete recovery with no sequelae"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "**Unilateral haemorrhagic parenchymal infarction usually results in hemiplegia** — the lesion is unilateral, so the motor deficit is unilateral.\n\nHaemorrhages occur in **25% of very-low-birthweight infants**, typically in the **germinal matrix above the caudate nucleus**, which contains a fragile network of blood vessels. **Most intraventricular haemorrhages occur within the first 72 hours of life.** Risk factors include **perinatal asphyxia, severe RDS, and pneumothorax**. Small haemorrhages remain confined to the germinal matrix; larger ones extend into the ventricles; the most severe is parenchymal haemorrhagic infarction.\n\nA large IVH may **impair drainage and reabsorption of CSF**, causing ventricular dilatation and hydrocephalus — separating cranial sutures, rapidly increasing head circumference, and producing a tense fontanelle. Of infants with progressive post-haemorrhagic ventricular dilatation, **half develop cerebral palsy**, and a higher proportion if there is parenchymal infarction. Management is symptomatic CSF removal by lumbar puncture initially, with a **ventriculoperitoneal or subgaleal shunt** if required. **Antenatal glucocorticoids reduce both the incidence and severity of IVH.**\n\n*(A)* **Periventricular leukomalacia** — bilateral multiple cysts from ischaemic white matter injury — carries an **80–90% risk of spastic diplegia**, often with cognitive impairment if posteriorly sited. Note that PVL may occur **without haemorrhage**.\n\n*(C)* Choreoathetoid cerebral palsy results from **kernicterus** (basal ganglia damage).\n\n**Both IVH and periventricular leukomalacia may occur in the absence of abnormal clinical signs**, which is why routine cranial ultrasound is performed.", "objective": "Germinal matrix haemorrhage with **unilateral parenchymal infarction → hemiplegia**. **Bilateral cystic PVL → spastic diplegia (80–90%)**. Most IVH occurs in the first 72 hours.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-53", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "neonatal", "stem": "A term infant delivered by elective caesarean section develops tachypnoea of 70 breaths/min with mild recession at 2 hours of age. He is otherwise well, pink in air, and afebrile. Chest X-ray shows fluid in the horizontal fissure.\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely diagnosis and expected course?", "options": ["Respiratory distress syndrome; requires surfactant", "Transient tachypnoea of the newborn; usually settles within the first day", "Meconium aspiration syndrome; requires mechanical ventilation", "Congenital pneumonia; requires 10 days of antibiotics", "Persistent pulmonary hypertension; requires inhaled nitric oxide"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "**Transient tachypnoea of the newborn (TTN) is the most common cause of respiratory distress in term infants.** It results from **delay in resorption of lung liquid**, and the classic setting is **elective caesarean section** — because the infant misses both the surge in maternal catecholamines during labour (which stimulates reabsorption of alveolar fluid) and the squeezing of the chest through the birth canal.\n\nOnset is **within the first day, typically around 2 hours**. Chest X-ray shows **fluid in the horizontal fissure**. It **usually settles within the first day** but can take several days to resolve completely. Management is supportive: **supplemental oxygen** and **nasogastric feeds or IV fluids** if the infant cannot feed normally. Diagnosis is made after **consideration and exclusion of other causes, particularly infection**.\n\nContrast with **RDS**: preterm, surfactant deficiency, onset **within 4 hours**, **ground-glass** X-ray, treated with surfactant and ventilation.\n\n*(C)* Meconium aspiration occurs in the context of meconium-stained liquor; X-ray shows **overinflated lungs with patchy collapse and consolidation**, with high incidence of air leak.\n\n*(E)* PPHN presents with **cyanosis soon after birth**, typically with normal heart size and pulmonary oligaemia on X-ray, treated with **inhaled nitric oxide, sildenafil, high-frequency ventilation, or ECMO**.", "objective": "TTN is the commonest cause of respiratory distress in term infants, classically after **elective caesarean section**, with **fluid in the horizontal fissure**, settling within the first day.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-54", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "neonatal", "stem": "A term infant is noted to be markedly jaundiced at 10 hours of age. The mother's blood group is O rhesus positive; the infant's is A rhesus positive. The direct antibody (Coombs) test is positive.\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely diagnosis, and what is the key principle regarding timing?", "options": ["Physiological jaundice; observation only", "ABO incompatibility; jaundice within 24 hours is usually haemolytic and needs urgent investigation", "Breast milk jaundice; reassurance", "Biliary atresia; urgent surgical referral", "Crigler–Najjar syndrome; lifelong phototherapy"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "**Jaundice starting within 24 hours of birth usually results from haemolysis** and requires **urgent investigation and close monitoring** — the bilirubin is unconjugated and **can rise very rapidly to extremely high levels**. This is one of the most important rules in neonatology.\n\nHere the mechanism is **ABO incompatibility**, now more common than rhesus haemolytic disease. Most ABO antibodies are IgM and do not cross the placenta, but **some group O women have IgG anti-A haemolysin** which crosses the placenta and haemolyses the red cells of a **group A infant** (occasionally group B infants via anti-B). Jaundice is severe but **usually less severe than rhesus disease**, typically peaking at **12–72 hours**, with **no hepatosplenomegaly** and a haemoglobin that is normal or only slightly reduced. The **direct antibody (Coombs) test is positive**.\n\nOther causes of jaundice **<24 hours**: rhesus incompatibility, **G6PD deficiency** (Mediterranean, Middle-Eastern, Far-Eastern, African origin; mainly males), spherocytosis, pyruvate kinase deficiency, and congenital infection (which is **conjugated**, with growth restriction, hepatosplenomegaly, and thrombocytopenic purpura).\n\n*(A, C)* Physiological and breast milk jaundice occur **after 24 hours**; \"physiological\" is a diagnosis of exclusion.\n\n*(D)* Biliary atresia is the critical diagnosis in **prolonged jaundice beyond 2 weeks** (3 weeks if preterm), presenting with **conjugated** hyperbilirubinaemia, **dark urine and pale unpigmented stools**.", "objective": "**Jaundice <24 hours of age is haemolytic until proven otherwise** and demands urgent investigation. ABO incompatibility: group O mother, group A/B infant, positive Coombs, no hepatosplenomegaly.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-55", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "neonatal", "stem": "A 5-week-old formerly preterm infant remains jaundiced. Investigations show a conjugated bilirubin of 60 µmol/L. His stools are pale and his urine is dark.\n\nWhich diagnosis must be excluded most urgently, and why?", "options": ["Breast milk jaundice, because it may last 12 weeks", "Congenital hypothyroidism, because of neurodevelopmental risk", "Biliary atresia, because delay in surgery adversely affects outcome", "G6PD deficiency, because of the risk of acute haemolysis", "Urinary tract infection, because of the risk of renal scarring"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "In **persistent (prolonged) neonatal jaundice** — beyond **2 weeks in term infants, 3 weeks in preterm** — the key feature is that it **may be caused by biliary atresia, and prompt diagnosis is essential because delay in surgical treatment adversely affects outcome.**\n\nThe decisive investigation is **whether the bilirubin is conjugated or unconjugated**. **Conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia (>25 µmol/L)** is always pathological and points to biliary atresia or neonatal hepatitis syndrome. The clinical signs supporting biliary atresia are exactly those described: **dark urine, unpigmented pale stools**, with hepatomegaly and poor weight gain.\n\nMost infants with prolonged jaundice have **unconjugated** hyperbilirubinaemia, the causes being:\n- **Breast milk jaundice** — the most common cause, affecting up to **15% of healthy breastfed infants**, fading by **12 weeks**\n- **Infection**, particularly **urinary tract infection**\n- **Congenital hypothyroidism**, which may cause prolonged jaundice before coarse facies, dry skin, hypotonia, and constipation appear — detected on **routine neonatal biochemical screening (Guthrie test)**\n\n*(A, B, E)* All are genuine causes of prolonged jaundice, but none is as time-critical as biliary atresia, and all produce **unconjugated** hyperbilirubinaemia — inconsistent with this infant's conjugated result.", "objective": "In jaundice persisting beyond **2 weeks (3 weeks if preterm)**, measure **conjugated bilirubin**. Conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia with **pale stools and dark urine = biliary atresia until excluded** — outcome depends on early surgery.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-56", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "neonatal", "stem": "A term infant with severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia becomes lethargic with poor feeding, then develops irritability, increased tone with an arched back, and seizures.\n\nWhich of the following long-term sequelae is most characteristic if the infant survives?", "options": ["Spastic diplegia and cortical blindness", "Choreoathetoid cerebral palsy and sensorineural deafness", "Hemiplegia and epilepsy", "Ataxic cerebral palsy and hydrocephalus", "Peripheral neuropathy and cataracts"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Kernicterus is the encephalopathy resulting from deposition of **unconjugated bilirubin in the basal ganglia and brainstem nuclei**. Survivors characteristically develop **choreoathetoid cerebral palsy (from basal ganglia damage) and sensorineural deafness**.\n\nThe mechanism: unconjugated bilirubin is **lipid-soluble and carried bound to albumin**. When the **albumin-binding capacity is exceeded**, free unconjugated bilirubin **crosses the blood–brain barrier**. This explains why drugs that **displace bilirubin from albumin — sulphonamides and diazepam — are avoided in newborns**, and why infants with hypoxia, hypothermia, acidosis, or low albumin are more susceptible.\n\nThe clinical progression described is classic: **lethargy and poor feeding → irritability and increased tone with opisthotonos → seizures and coma**.\n\nKernicterus became rare after **prophylactic anti-D immunoglobulin** for rhesus-negative mothers, but cases still occur, particularly in **slightly preterm infants (35–37 weeks)** and **dark-skin-toned infants in whom jaundice is harder to detect**.\n\nTreatment of hyperbilirubinaemia: **phototherapy** using light at **450 nm from the blue-green band**, converting unconjugated bilirubin to a water-soluble pigment excreted mainly in urine. Complications include temperature instability, macular rash, and **bronze discoloration if the jaundice is conjugated**. **Exchange transfusion** — replacing **twice the infant's blood volume (2 × 90 mL/kg)** with fresh, screened donor blood — is used if bilirubin reaches dangerous levels. In rhesus or ABO disease unresponsive to intensive phototherapy, **IV immunoglobulin reduces the need for exchange transfusion**.", "objective": "Kernicterus deposits unconjugated bilirubin in **basal ganglia and brainstem**, causing **choreoathetoid cerebral palsy and sensorineural deafness**. Free (albumin-unbound) bilirubin crosses the blood–brain barrier.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-57", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "neonatal", "stem": "A term infant born after prolonged rupture of membranes develops respiratory distress and temperature instability at 18 hours of age.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate empirical antibiotic regimen, and what is the correct interpretation of C-reactive protein in this setting?", "options": ["Vancomycin plus meropenem; a single normal CRP excludes infection", "Benzylpenicillin plus gentamicin; CRP takes 12–24 hours to rise, so one normal result does not exclude infection", "Flucloxacillin plus gentamicin; CRP rises immediately and is highly specific", "Cefotaxime alone; CRP is not useful in neonates", "Ampicillin alone; CRP should be checked only after 72 hours"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "This is **early-onset sepsis (<72 hours after birth)**, acquired vertically by **ascending infection or exposure to a high bacterial load during birth after rupture of the membranes**. The organisms are **group B streptococcus** and **Gram-negative organisms (E. coli, Klebsiella, Pseudomonas)** commonly, with **Listeria monocytogenes** rarely.\n\nThe empirical combination reflects this spectrum: **benzylpenicillin or ampicillin** (covering group B streptococcus, Listeria, and other Gram-positives) plus an **aminoglycoside such as gentamicin** (covering Gram-negatives). **Antibiotics are started immediately without waiting for culture results.**\n\n**CRP takes 12–24 hours to rise, so one normal result does not exclude infection — but two consecutive normal values are strong evidence against it.** If cultures and CRP are negative and there are no clinical indicators, **antibiotics are stopped after 36–48 hours**.\n\n*(C)* **Flucloxacillin plus gentamicin** is the regimen for **late-onset sepsis (>72 hours)**, where the source is the infant's environment — indwelling central venous catheters, invasive procedures, tracheal tubes — and the commonest pathogen is **coagulase-negative staphylococcus**. If resistant or not improving, **vancomycin** (for CoNS or enterococci) or **meropenem** is used, with the caveat that prolonged broad-spectrum antibiotics predispose to **invasive fungal infection (Candida)**.\n\nFor **neonatal meningitis**, treatment is **ampicillin or penicillin plus a third-generation cephalosporin (cefotaxime, which penetrates CSF)**.", "objective": "Early-onset sepsis (<72 h): **GBS, E. coli, Listeria** → **benzylpenicillin + gentamicin**. Late-onset (>72 h): **CoNS** → flucloxacillin + gentamicin. **A single normal CRP does not exclude sepsis; two consecutive normal values largely do.**", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-58", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "neonatal", "stem": "A 39-week infant is delivered after a placental abruption. Apgar scores were 2 at 1 minute and 4 at 5 minutes. At 3 hours of age he is markedly hypotonic, cannot suck, has had two brief apnoeas, and has had a seizure.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate specific therapy, and within what time frame must it begin?", "options": ["Therapeutic hypothermia to 33–34°C for 72 hours, started within 6 hours of birth", "Therapeutic hypothermia to 30°C for 24 hours, started within 24 hours of birth", "Prophylactic phenobarbitone alone; cooling is contraindicated with seizures", "Active warming to 38°C to improve cerebral perfusion", "Hyperbaric oxygen therapy within 12 hours"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "This infant has **moderate (Sarnat grade 2) hypoxic–ischaemic encephalopathy** — marked abnormalities of movement, hypotonia, inability to suck, brief apnoeas, and seizures. **Therapeutic hypothermia** is indicated: cooling to a **rectal temperature of 33–34°C for 72 hours**, **started within 6 hours of birth**, in infants **≥36 weeks' gestation** with **moderate or severe HIE**. Hyperthermia (>37.5°C) must be avoided.\n\nThe rationale rests on the biphasic nature of the injury: neuronal damage may be **immediate (primary neuronal death)** or **delayed, from reperfusion injury causing secondary neuronal death from secondary energy failure**. **This delay creates the therapeutic window** for neuroprotection.\n\nEfficacy: **for every eight babies cooled, one extra baby survives without disability** (NNT = 8). Trials show reduction in death or major disability, mortality, major neurodisability, and cerebral palsy in survivors — though **reduction in seizures is not demonstrated**.\n\nSarnat grading and prognosis:\n- **Mild (grade 1):** irritable, excessive response to stimulation, staring, hyperventilation → **complete recovery expected**\n- **Moderate (grade 2):** hypotonia, cannot feed, brief apnoeas, seizures → full recovery likely **if neurologically normal and feeding by 2 weeks**; if abnormalities persist beyond that, poor prognosis\n- **Severe (grade 3):** no spontaneous movements, no response to pain, prolonged refractory seizures, multi-organ failure → **30–40% mortality without cooling; 80% of survivors have neurodevelopmental disability**\n\nSupportive management includes respiratory support, anticonvulsants, **fluid restriction** (transient renal impairment and SIADH), treatment of hypotension, and monitoring for **hypoglycaemia and hypocalcaemia**. **aEEG** confirms encephalopathy and identifies seizures. MRI at **5–14 days** showing significant abnormality predicts high risk of later cerebral palsy.", "objective": "Therapeutic hypothermia for **moderate or severe HIE**: **33–34°C rectal for 72 hours, within 6 hours of birth, ≥36 weeks' gestation**. NNT = 8 for survival without disability.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-59", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "neonatal", "stem": "A newborn delivered by ventouse has a scalp swelling noted at 6 hours of age. On examination the swelling is confined within the margins of the parietal bone and does not cross the suture lines. The centre feels soft.\n\nWhich of the following is the diagnosis and expected course?", "options": ["Caput succedaneum; resolves in a few days", "Cephalhaematoma; resolves over several weeks", "Subaponeurotic haemorrhage; risk of hypovolaemic shock", "Craniosynostosis; requires surgical correction", "Encephalocele; requires urgent neurosurgical referral"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "A **cephalhaematoma** is a haematoma from **bleeding below the periosteum**. The defining feature is that it is **confined within the margins of the skull sutures** — because the periosteum is adherent at suture lines — and it **usually involves the parietal bone**. The centre feels soft. It **resolves over several weeks**.\n\nThe three extracranial haemorrhages must be distinguished:\n\n**Caput succedaneum** — **bruising and oedema of the presenting part, extending beyond the margins of the skull bones** (it crosses sutures because it lies in subcutaneous tissue above the aponeurosis). **Resolves in a few days.**\n\n**Cephalhaematoma** — subperiosteal, **does not cross sutures**, resolves over weeks.\n\n**Subaponeurotic (subgaleal) haemorrhage** — **very uncommon but dangerous**: a **diffuse, boggy swelling of the scalp** in the potential space beneath the aponeurosis, where **blood loss may be severe, leading to hypovolaemic shock and coagulopathy**.\n\nThe clinical importance of the distinction is entirely about the third: caput and cephalhaematoma are benign, while subaponeurotic haemorrhage can exsanguinate an infant.\n\nOther birth injuries worth knowing: **chignon** (oedema and bruising from ventouse), forceps marks, and **Erb palsy** from traction on the **upper nerve roots (C5 and C6)** at breech delivery or shoulder dystocia — the arm lies straight and limp with the hand pronated and fingers flexed (**\"waiter's tip\" position**), sometimes with phrenic nerve palsy causing an elevated diaphragm. Erb palsy usually resolves completely but should be referred if unresolved by **2–3 months**.", "objective": "**Caput succedaneum crosses suture lines** (resolves in days); **cephalhaematoma does not** (resolves in weeks); **subaponeurotic haemorrhage is diffuse and boggy** and may cause hypovolaemic shock.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-60", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "neonatal", "stem": "A term infant born to a mother with gestational diabetes has a blood glucose of 1.8 mmol/L at 2 hours of age. He is asymptomatic.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": ["Observe and recheck in 6 hours", "Give dextrose gel to the mouth and recheck after 30 minutes", "Immediate intravenous 10% dextrose via peripheral cannula", "Intramuscular glucagon", "Withhold feeds and start parenteral nutrition"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "For a blood glucose **below 2 mmol/L without clinical signs**, the recommended step is **dextrose gel administered to the mouth with a recheck after 30 minutes**, alongside feeding. This avoids unnecessary intravenous access in an otherwise well infant.\n\nThe UK (British Association of Perinatal Medicine) thresholds:\n- **2–2.6 mmol/L** — feed and recheck until satisfactory (**at least 3 pre-feed levels >2 mmol/L**)\n- **<2 mmol/L** (asymptomatic) — **dextrose gel**, recheck after 30 minutes\n- **<2.0 mmol/L with clinical signs, or <1 mmol/L** — **intravenous dextrose**\n\nBlood glucose **above 2.6 mmol/L** is considered desirable for optimal neurodevelopmental outcome, though many asymptomatic infants transiently fall below this in the first 24 hours. There is **no agreed definition of neonatal hypoglycaemia**.\n\nCritically, if IV dextrose is required at **high concentration (15% or 20%)**, it must be given **via a central venous catheter** to avoid **extravasation causing skin necrosis**, and abrupt cessation risks **reactive hypoglycaemia**. **Glucagon** is reserved for no satisfactory response or difficulty/delay establishing an infusion.\n\nMechanism in this infant: exposure to elevated maternal glucose causes **hyperplasia of pancreatic islet cells** and **raised insulin levels**. Other at-risk groups are those with **poor glycogen stores** — IUGR, preterm, hypothermic, polycythaemic, or ill infants for any reason.\n\nSymptoms are **jitteriness, irritability, apnoea, lethargy, drowsiness, and seizures**. **Prolonged symptomatic hypoglycaemia can cause permanent neurological disability**, and prevention is by **early and frequent milk feeding** with regular pre-feed bedside monitoring in at-risk infants.", "objective": "Neonatal hypoglycaemia: **2–2.6 mmol/L** → feed and recheck; **<2 mmol/L asymptomatic** → **dextrose gel** and recheck at 30 minutes; **<2 with signs or <1 mmol/L** → IV dextrose (central line if ≥15%).", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-61", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "allergy", "stem": "A 6-month-old exclusively breastfed infant develops widespread urticaria and lip swelling within 15 minutes of his first formula feed. Skin-prick testing to cow's milk produces an 8 mm weal.\n\nWhich of the following statements about the interpretation of this result is correct?", "options": ["A weal of 4 mm or greater is considered positive, and this confirms sensitisation", "Any visible weal is positive regardless of size", "Skin-prick tests are unreliable under 1 year of age", "Antihistamines taken within 48–72 hours would produce a false positive", "Sensitisation on skin-prick testing is synonymous with clinical allergy"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "**Weals of 4 mm or greater are considered positive.** A drop of allergen (extract or fresh food) is placed on the skin, the site marked, the skin pricked with a lancet, and **weals measured after 15 minutes**.\n\nThe distinction the question ultimately tests is between **sensitisation** and **allergy**. **Sensitisation is a positive test to an allergen — by skin-prick test or specific IgE — and does not equate to allergy unless a clinical reaction occurs on exposure.** In this infant both are present: a positive test *and* an unequivocal clinical reaction, so the diagnosis is IgE-mediated cow's milk allergy. The higher the number of positive tests, the more likely a person is to be allergic.\n\n*(D)* Antihistamines must be **stopped 48–72 hours before testing**; if taken, they cause **false negatives**, not false positives. Provided they have been stopped, negative tests suggest allergy is unlikely.\n\n*(E)* This is the central error — a positive test alone is sensitisation, not allergy.\n\nKey definitions worth committing:\n- **Hypersensitivity** — objectively reproducible symptoms or signs following exposure to a defined stimulus at a dose usually tolerated by most people\n- **Allergy** — a hypersensitivity reaction initiated by specific **immunological** mechanisms; may be **IgE-mediated (peanut allergy)** or **non-IgE-mediated (coeliac disease)**\n- **Atopy** — a personal or familial tendency to produce **IgE antibodies** in response to ordinary allergen exposure\n- **Immune tolerance** — absence of an active immune response against a particular antigen\n\nFor blood testing, **raised total IgE indicates atopy**, but **specific IgE** is needed for individual allergens, and **foods the child already eats without symptoms should not be tested** as this may yield false positives.", "objective": "Skin-prick weal **≥4 mm is positive**. **Sensitisation ≠ allergy** — a positive test requires a corresponding clinical reaction to diagnose allergy. Stop antihistamines 48–72 hours before testing.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-62", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "allergy", "stem": "A 4-month-old formula-fed infant has loose stools and faltering growth. Skin-prick testing to cow's milk shows a 0 mm weal. Elimination of cow's milk resolves the symptoms, which return on trial reintroduction.\n\nWhich of the following best describes this condition?", "options": ["IgE-mediated cow's milk allergy", "Non-IgE-mediated cow's milk allergy", "Lactose intolerance", "Food aversion", "Coeliac disease"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "This is **non-IgE-mediated cow's milk allergy**. The negative skin-prick test excludes an IgE mechanism, but the reproducible relationship between elimination and reintroduction establishes that this is an **immunological** response to food protein — hence allergy rather than intolerance.\n\nThe two mechanisms differ clinically in ways that are highly examinable:\n\n**IgE-mediated food allergy** — onset **10–15 minutes (up to 2 hours)** after ingestion, with urticaria, facial swelling, or anaphylaxis. There is a characteristic **early phase within minutes** (histamine and other mast cell mediators, causing urticaria, angioedema, sneezing, vomiting, bronchospasm, cardiovascular shock) and a **late phase after 4–6 hours** (nasal congestion, cough, bronchospasm). **The late phase is precisely why children are observed in hospital for several hours after a moderate or severe food reaction.** The majority of severe life-threatening reactions are IgE-mediated.\n\n**Non-IgE-mediated food allergy** — **delayed onset over hours**, with a more varied course, typically **gastrointestinal**: diarrhoea, vomiting, abdominal pain, and sometimes faltering growth. It may present with **blood in the stools in the first weeks of life from proctitis**, or rarely with severe repetitive vomiting after milk or rice progressing to shock (**food protein-induced enterocolitis syndrome**).\n\n*(C)* **Lactose intolerance** is a **non-immunological hypersensitivity** — a food *intolerance*, not an allergy. The classic vignette is a previously well infant who develops gastroenteritis, after which watery stools persist for weeks, with **stool positive for reducing substances and no pathogens**.\n\n*(D)* Food aversion is refusal of food for psychological or behavioural reasons.", "objective": "IgE-mediated food allergy = rapid (minutes), urticaria/anaphylaxis, positive skin-prick test. Non-IgE-mediated = **delayed (hours), gastrointestinal, negative skin-prick test**. Food intolerance (e.g. lactose) is **non-immunological**.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-63", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "allergy", "stem": "A 3-year-old with known egg allergy has avoided all egg products. His mother reports that he ate a slice of birthday cake at nursery last week with no reaction at all, and is confused.\n\nWhich of the following best explains this observation?", "options": ["He has outgrown his egg allergy completely", "Heat degradation during baking alters the protein epitope, making the food less allergenic", "The cake contained egg substitute", "His previous reactions were non-allergic in origin", "Concurrent antihistamine use masked the reaction"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "**Proteins with an unstable tertiary structure may be rendered non-allergenic by heat degradation or other processing.** In milk and egg allergy specifically, **many children tolerate egg or milk in a baked form**, because cooking at high temperatures **changes the protein epitope** and makes the food less allergenic. This is why a child may react to runny egg yet tolerate cooked egg or egg in baked goods.\n\nThis has real clinical utility — it widens the diet considerably and may aid the development of tolerance — but reintroduction should be structured rather than assumed from a single accidental exposure.\n\n*(A)* Possible in principle, since **cow's milk and egg allergy often resolve in early childhood** and gradual reintroduction under dietetic supervision may be feasible — but a single tolerated baked exposure does not establish resolution of allergy to raw/lightly cooked egg. By contrast, **allergy to nuts and seafood usually persists into adulthood**.\n\n*(C, D, E)* None explains the well-recognised baked-food phenomenon.\n\nTwo related management points: when one food allergy is diagnosed, **related allergies must be considered** — **20% of children with egg allergy have peanut sensitisation**, and children with peanut allergy often have tree nut allergy. And the diagnosis of food allergy **should not be made lightly**, as dietary restriction and fear of accidental reactions have a major impact on family life.", "objective": "**Baked (heat-processed) egg and milk are often tolerated** by children allergic to the raw forms, because high-temperature cooking alters the protein epitope. Milk and egg allergy commonly resolve; nut and seafood allergy usually persist.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-64", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "allergy", "stem": "A 10-year-old girl who tolerated apples throughout childhood now develops an itchy mouth and mild lip tingling whenever she eats a raw apple. She has recently developed seasonal rhinoconjunctivitis in spring. Systemic symptoms have never occurred.\n\nWhich of the following best explains her symptoms?", "options": ["Primary IgE-mediated apple allergy", "Pollen food allergy syndrome from cross-reactivity with birch pollen", "Oral candidiasis", "Food protein-induced enterocolitis syndrome", "Hereditary angioedema"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "This is **pollen food allergy syndrome**, a **secondary** food allergy arising from **cross-reactivity between proteins in fresh fruits, vegetables, or nuts and those present in pollens**. The textbook example is exactly this scenario: a child who can eat apples develops apple allergy when older **because they have become allergic to birch tree pollen**, and apple and birch pollen share a very similar protein.\n\nThe clinical hallmarks are reassuring and characteristic: it **generally leads to mild allergic reactions, often causing an itchy mouth, but rarely systemic symptoms**. The temporal pattern also fits — the seasonal rhinoconjunctivitis signals the primary pollen sensitisation that drives the cross-reactivity.\n\n*(A)* **Primary food allergy** is the more common pattern, in which **children usually react on first exposure**. This girl tolerated apples for years, which points away from primary allergy.\n\nThe age distribution of causative foods is worth knowing: **in infants — egg, cow's milk, and nuts**; **in older children — peanut, tree nut, fish, shellfish, and sesame**. Fruits, legumes, and seeds are also common causes of IgE-mediated food allergy. **Over 80% of children with food allergy present in the first year of life**, and many have atopic eczema.\n\n*(E)* **Angioedema without urticaria** suggests **C1 esterase inhibitor deficiency (hereditary angioedema)**, a very rare disorder of complement cascade control requiring specialist review — but this presentation is oral itching related to a specific food, not isolated angioedema.", "objective": "**Pollen food allergy syndrome** — cross-reactivity between pollen and fresh fruit/vegetable/nut proteins, presenting later in childhood with **oral itching but rarely systemic reactions**. Contrast with primary food allergy, where reactions occur on first exposure.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}, {"id": "p1-65", "module": "pediatrics", "chapter": "allergy", "stem": "A 2-year-old boy has severe atopic eczema affecting the limb flexures from 3 months of age. His mother asks why he has since developed both a peanut allergy and asthma.\n\nMutation in which gene most directly links these conditions?", "options": ["FLG (filaggrin)", "MEFV (pyrin)", "CFTR", "SHOX", "ADAM33"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "**Filaggrin (FLG) gene mutations are the key genetic risk factor for eczema development**, through **impairment of skin barrier function**. The impaired barrier permits **cutaneous sensitisation to inhalant and food allergens** — which is why **filaggrin mutations predispose not only to eczema but also to food allergy, asthma, and hay fever**. This mechanism elegantly explains the clustering the mother has observed.\n\nThe broader principle: **intact barrier function of the skin appears to be important** in preventing allergic disease, and allergy is an **epigenetic condition** in which environmental exposures interact with susceptibility genes. The developing immune system must be **sensitised** to an allergen before an allergic response develops.\n\nSupporting facts: affected children have a family history of allergy, **at least 50% develop other allergic diseases**, and IgE antibodies to common allergens are usually present. **Up to 40% of young infants with severe eczema have an IgE-mediated food allergy** — a strong argument for considering food allergy assessment in this group.\n\nThe typical **age sequence of atopic disease** is examinable: **eczema and food allergy develop in infancy**; **allergic rhinitis, conjunctivitis, and asthma begin in preschool and primary school years**. Allergic disorders coexist — **most children with food allergy have a history of early-onset eczema, and most children with asthma have some degree of allergic rhinitis**. If a child has one allergic disease, look for others.\n\n*(B)* MEFV causes familial Mediterranean fever. *(C)* CFTR causes cystic fibrosis. *(D)* SHOX abnormalities cause short stature.", "objective": "**Filaggrin mutations impair skin barrier function**, causing eczema and permitting cutaneous sensitisation — predisposing to **food allergy, asthma, and allergic rhinitis**. Up to 40% of infants with severe eczema have IgE-mediated food allergy.", "source": "Pediatrics Part 1"}];

